1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:19,600
OK, so let's start back up.

2
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:20,640
Today, let's see.

3
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:24,240
We are transitioning from morphology into phonetics.

4
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:26,080
So I hope you enjoyed morphology.

5
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:28,520
It's not as if you won't ever do any morphology again,

6
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:33,280
but that's it for lessons on morphology, lectures, I guess.

7
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:35,560
Today is phonetics, which means that today we

8
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:37,760
begin making funny sounds at each other.

9
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:42,120
So everybody limber up your vocal tracks.

10
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:42,600
Let's see.

11
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:44,320
I'm trying to remember if there's anything

12
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:46,160
that I ought to announce.

13
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:48,840
You remember maybe that problem set one, which

14
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:53,040
confusingly is your second problem set, is due on Thursday.

15
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:54,640
Normally it would be due on Tuesday,

16
00:00:54,640 --> 00:00:58,480
but because I am technologically challenged,

17
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:01,240
it's due on Thursday.

18
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:02,760
Speaking of being technologically challenged,

19
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:05,360
I just figured out how to get the projector to project

20
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:06,880
over there instead of in the middle

21
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:08,880
so that I won't have to write everything twice running back

22
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:09,920
and forth across the room.

23
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:10,640
So thank you.

24
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:16,720
I'm going to mention this when I go up for, you know,

25
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:19,160
my chair is trying to decide whether to give me a raise.

26
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:22,080
I'll be like, big academic achievements of the year.

27
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:24,040
Figuring out the techniques.

28
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:25,840
So if anybody misses the old days,

29
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:27,880
like you were used watching me run back and forth,

30
00:01:27,880 --> 00:01:32,040
or this turns out to be too small or something like that,

31
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:34,160
let me know, and we'll go back to the old days.

32
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:43,360
So when we speak, if we're speaking an oral language,

33
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:47,360
if we're not signing, what we are typically doing

34
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:50,400
is producing a flow of air, which typically comes out

35
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:52,040
of your lungs, but not necessarily.

36
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:54,080
We'll talk about that.

37
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:58,720
And it gets obstructed in various ways in the vocal tract.

38
00:01:58,720 --> 00:02:01,160
And so one standard way of talking

39
00:02:01,160 --> 00:02:03,000
about different kinds of speech sounds

40
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,920
is to talk about where in the vocal tract things the flow

41
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:09,920
of air can get obstructed and how.

42
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:12,520
And that's what we're going to kind of do.

43
00:02:12,520 --> 00:02:14,160
So first, we'll talk about where.

44
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:17,040
So one way of categorizing the various things

45
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:20,920
that your vocal tract does to the airflow

46
00:02:20,920 --> 00:02:22,840
is by what's called place of articulation.

47
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:25,080
That is, where in your vocal tract

48
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:28,240
is the flow of air getting obstructed.

49
00:02:28,240 --> 00:02:32,480
So for example, there are what are called bilabial sounds.

50
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:35,520
These are sounds which are made with both lips.

51
00:02:35,520 --> 00:02:37,040
This picture over here on the right

52
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:38,720
is what's called a sagittal section.

53
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:42,640
That is, it's a picture of someone's head cut in half

54
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:46,280
so that you can see the stuff that's inside.

55
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:48,800
And those arrows are meant to get you

56
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:51,600
to imagine that this person is making a sound

57
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:53,320
by putting their two lips together.

58
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:54,920
So that's what you do for the sounds

59
00:02:54,920 --> 00:02:58,360
that are at the beginnings of words like paint and bath

60
00:02:58,360 --> 00:03:02,160
and mouth and, well, wipe, where your lips don't touch,

61
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:04,440
but they both move.

62
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:06,800
Everybody, feel free to confirm to yourselves

63
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:09,920
the privacy of your own mask that that is what you're

64
00:03:09,920 --> 00:03:11,840
doing when you make these sounds.

65
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:16,800
Now, next to the sounds, so I have these words, paint, bath,

66
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:18,520
and wipe, and then next to them I

67
00:03:18,520 --> 00:03:21,720
have these symbols in brackets.

68
00:03:21,720 --> 00:03:23,080
And they may not look at it, but these

69
00:03:23,080 --> 00:03:24,600
are extremely technical symbols.

70
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:27,920
These are symbols of the international phonetic alphabet.

71
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:31,360
So linguists have a system for writing sounds down

72
00:03:31,360 --> 00:03:33,960
so that we'll all know what kind of sound we're talking about

73
00:03:33,960 --> 00:03:36,960
when we're talking about sounds.

74
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:39,840
And bless you, a lot of the symbols

75
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:41,400
of the international phonetic alphabet

76
00:03:41,400 --> 00:03:43,320
resemble letters of the English alphabet.

77
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:45,040
So we have started here with symbols

78
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:46,520
that should all look kind of familiar.

79
00:03:46,520 --> 00:03:50,200
So the symbol for the sound at the beginning of paint

80
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:53,760
is the letter P. And so that's a symbol

81
00:03:53,760 --> 00:03:55,440
of the international phonetic alphabet.

82
00:03:55,440 --> 00:03:57,400
As we go along, we will be seeing weirder and weirder

83
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:59,360
symbols from the international phonetic alphabet.

84
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:01,560
So please enjoy it while it's easy.

85
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:02,760
So far so good.

86
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:10,160
There are also what are called labiodental sounds.

87
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:14,240
Labiodental sounds involve your top teeth and your lower lip.

88
00:04:14,240 --> 00:04:15,640
If you think about what you're doing

89
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:20,280
at the beginning of a sound like face or vase,

90
00:04:20,280 --> 00:04:22,520
what you're doing, at least if you're me,

91
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:26,520
is bringing your lower lip pretty close to your upper teeth

92
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:28,360
and obstructing the flow of air there

93
00:04:28,360 --> 00:04:31,400
so that there's some turbulence.

94
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:35,680
There are, I believe, no languages in which labiodentals

95
00:04:35,680 --> 00:04:38,600
are made with your bottom teeth and your upper lip.

96
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:41,880
It's a little hard to do face, face.

97
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:44,680
We'll talk about other kinds of places of articulation

98
00:04:44,680 --> 00:04:45,640
that English doesn't use.

99
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:48,480
But I think that one just doesn't exist.

100
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:50,440
And again, two symbols of the IPA,

101
00:04:50,440 --> 00:04:52,840
which, again, are not going to be too hard for you to learn.

102
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:55,840
Yeah, so the symbol for the sound at the beginning of face

103
00:04:55,840 --> 00:04:58,120
is an F. The symbol for the sound at the beginning of vase

104
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:02,920
is a V. Here we get our first two IPA symbols that

105
00:05:02,920 --> 00:05:05,920
are not letters of the English alphabet.

106
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:08,360
These are interdental sounds.

107
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:10,800
Interdental sounds are cross linguistically not hugely

108
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:12,120
common.

109
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:14,000
We have them in English.

110
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,960
There are various dialects of Arabic that have them.

111
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:19,440
Some of the Berber languages have them.

112
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:22,960
But they're not all that common.

113
00:05:22,960 --> 00:05:25,040
These are the sounds at the beginnings of words

114
00:05:25,040 --> 00:05:28,320
like thistle and this, where you're

115
00:05:28,320 --> 00:05:30,920
sticking your tongue between your front teeth

116
00:05:30,920 --> 00:05:33,360
and making air flow out.

117
00:05:36,860 --> 00:05:40,480
English doesn't spell these differently, at least not

118
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:41,480
reliably.

119
00:05:41,520 --> 00:05:43,560
So we spell both of these with a th.

120
00:05:43,560 --> 00:05:46,120
That's one of the reasons the International Fanatic Alphabet

121
00:05:46,120 --> 00:05:48,320
is there, so that we can unambiguously talk

122
00:05:48,320 --> 00:05:49,520
about what we're talking about.

123
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:51,760
So the sound at the beginning of thistle

124
00:05:51,760 --> 00:05:53,960
and the sound at the beginning of this,

125
00:05:53,960 --> 00:05:55,200
they're not the same sound.

126
00:05:55,200 --> 00:05:57,640
We'll talk about the difference between them shortly.

127
00:05:57,640 --> 00:05:59,480
But at this point, maybe it's just clear,

128
00:05:59,480 --> 00:06:00,440
they're not the same sound.

129
00:06:00,440 --> 00:06:02,640
So there are two different IPA symbols for them.

130
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:04,480
The Greek letter theta is used for the sound

131
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:06,240
at the beginning of thistle.

132
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:09,040
And that second letter is an old English letter.

133
00:06:09,040 --> 00:06:10,320
It's still used in Icelandic.

134
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:12,840
It's sometimes called ev, and it

135
00:06:12,840 --> 00:06:14,760
stands for the sound at the beginning of this.

136
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:25,080
Then there are what are called alveolar sounds.

137
00:06:25,080 --> 00:06:31,400
If you put your tongue at the top of your mouth

138
00:06:31,400 --> 00:06:35,480
and kind of drag it, so put it first against your front teeth,

139
00:06:35,480 --> 00:06:37,640
and then kind of drag it backwards

140
00:06:37,640 --> 00:06:39,800
along the top of your mouth.

141
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:44,400
If you're like me, you've got your teeth,

142
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:47,360
and then there's this kind of flat-ish space,

143
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:49,160
and then it kind of goes up.

144
00:06:49,160 --> 00:06:53,120
So from your teeth, you go back, there's a little plateau.

145
00:06:53,120 --> 00:06:55,200
There's this little plateau there right behind your teeth,

146
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:59,720
this gum ridge, and then your mouth begins to go up higher.

147
00:06:59,720 --> 00:07:03,080
That ridge is called the alveolar ridge.

148
00:07:03,080 --> 00:07:05,400
And if you put your tongue there,

149
00:07:05,400 --> 00:07:07,520
then you are all set to make sounds

150
00:07:07,560 --> 00:07:12,800
like the sound at the beginning of the word teeth or duck

151
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:14,320
or nail.

152
00:07:14,320 --> 00:07:16,800
And for sounds like sail and zoom,

153
00:07:16,800 --> 00:07:19,000
again, you're not actually touching that position.

154
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,400
We'll talk more about the difference between S and T

155
00:07:21,400 --> 00:07:22,320
in just a second.

156
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:23,880
But they're both alveolar sounds.

157
00:07:23,880 --> 00:07:25,920
Your tongue is pointing in the general direction

158
00:07:25,920 --> 00:07:28,200
of your alveolar ridge.

159
00:07:28,200 --> 00:07:29,680
Everybody have that sensation?

160
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:32,680
You should all be exploring the insides of your own heads

161
00:07:32,680 --> 00:07:33,180
right now.

162
00:07:38,520 --> 00:07:41,800
There's a type of sound that has been called alveopalital.

163
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:43,080
It's also called post-alveolar.

164
00:07:43,080 --> 00:07:45,080
I'll call it post-alveolar.

165
00:07:45,080 --> 00:07:48,400
If you think first about an S, and then you

166
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:52,280
think about a shh, the shh at the beginning of shit.

167
00:07:52,280 --> 00:07:55,240
If you go back and forth between them,

168
00:07:55,240 --> 00:07:58,840
shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh.

169
00:07:58,840 --> 00:08:02,000
You make a very soothing sound.

170
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:02,040
Yeah?

171
00:08:02,040 --> 00:08:04,440
And maybe what you can feel is that your tongue

172
00:08:04,720 --> 00:08:07,080
and maybe what you can feel is that your tongue

173
00:08:07,080 --> 00:08:09,000
is kind of rocking back and forth.

174
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:10,680
At least that's what my tongue is doing.

175
00:08:10,680 --> 00:08:13,840
For S, it's kind of pointing at the front,

176
00:08:13,840 --> 00:08:15,160
at my alveolar ridge.

177
00:08:15,160 --> 00:08:18,000
And then for shh, the middle of my tongue

178
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,680
kind of curls up a little bit, goes a little further back.

179
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:23,200
Is that the feeling people are having?

180
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:25,200
Is there going back and forth between these?

181
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:28,120
Here's another new symbol.

182
00:08:28,120 --> 00:08:30,880
That's the symbol for the sound at the beginning of ship.

183
00:08:30,880 --> 00:08:33,080
And another new symbol, that's the sound

184
00:08:33,120 --> 00:08:37,520
in the middle of azure, the shh sound.

185
00:08:37,520 --> 00:08:39,760
Both of those are post-alveolar sounds.

186
00:08:39,760 --> 00:08:42,240
You'll sometimes see them called alveopalital sounds.

187
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:45,240
I'll try to remember to always call them post-alveolar.

188
00:08:45,240 --> 00:08:45,740
Yep.

189
00:08:48,520 --> 00:08:50,240
A little further back, there are

190
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:53,640
what are called palatal sounds.

191
00:08:53,640 --> 00:08:55,840
These are even further behind the alveolar ridge,

192
00:08:55,840 --> 00:08:57,920
sort of back where the roof of your mouth

193
00:08:57,920 --> 00:08:59,680
gets as high as it's going to get.

194
00:08:59,680 --> 00:09:02,240
And the one palatal sound that we have in English

195
00:09:02,240 --> 00:09:05,120
is the y sound at the beginning of year.

196
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:07,400
And here's the first IPA symbol that is deliberately

197
00:09:07,400 --> 00:09:09,520
designed to confuse you.

198
00:09:09,520 --> 00:09:12,560
The IPA symbol for that sound, the y at the beginning of year,

199
00:09:12,560 --> 00:09:14,240
is a j.

200
00:09:14,240 --> 00:09:18,400
This is because the IPA was not invented exclusively

201
00:09:18,400 --> 00:09:19,840
by speakers of English.

202
00:09:19,840 --> 00:09:22,320
So it was invented also by speakers of languages

203
00:09:22,320 --> 00:09:26,800
like German, in which they used this letter for this sound.

204
00:09:26,800 --> 00:09:29,920
Ja, for example, the German word for yes, it's spelled ja.

205
00:09:33,240 --> 00:09:35,120
OK.

206
00:09:35,120 --> 00:09:36,960
The letter y is used for something else.

207
00:09:36,960 --> 00:09:37,520
We'll get to it.

208
00:09:40,280 --> 00:09:43,080
And then, sort of continuing our tour of the mouth.

209
00:09:43,080 --> 00:09:45,120
So we're working our way backwards through the mouth.

210
00:09:45,120 --> 00:09:47,240
There are what are called velar sounds.

211
00:09:47,240 --> 00:09:50,120
In the velar sound, the body of your tongue

212
00:09:50,120 --> 00:09:52,080
is up against what's called the velum, which

213
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:53,960
is the soft tissue at the back of your mouth

214
00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:55,960
that we're going to be hearing more about in a second.

215
00:09:55,960 --> 00:09:58,280
It's responsible for partitioning your mouth

216
00:09:58,280 --> 00:10:02,360
from your nose, from your oral cavity.

217
00:10:02,360 --> 00:10:05,800
So that's the place that your tongue is touching

218
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:07,840
when you say the sounds at the beginnings of words

219
00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:12,280
like colonel and caught and gone.

220
00:10:12,280 --> 00:10:14,920
And when you make the nasal sound at the end of a word

221
00:10:14,920 --> 00:10:19,680
like sing, so if you think about where your tongue is,

222
00:10:19,680 --> 00:10:22,160
it's up in the back there.

223
00:10:22,160 --> 00:10:24,720
Does that sound right?

224
00:10:24,720 --> 00:10:27,280
Yeah?

225
00:10:27,720 --> 00:10:30,720
Cool.

226
00:10:30,720 --> 00:10:34,520
And then, going even further down.

227
00:10:34,520 --> 00:10:36,520
Further down in your throat, you've

228
00:10:36,520 --> 00:10:39,880
got the vocal cords of the glottis, this space that's

229
00:10:39,880 --> 00:10:42,720
down there in your throat around your larynx.

230
00:10:42,720 --> 00:10:46,040
And your vocal cords, or they're sometimes

231
00:10:46,040 --> 00:10:47,680
called your vocal folds.

232
00:10:47,680 --> 00:10:50,000
This process that you've got back here in your throat

233
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:53,760
for closing off the air can be closed

234
00:10:53,760 --> 00:10:58,080
to make what's called a glottal stop.

235
00:10:58,080 --> 00:11:02,880
English doesn't make a huge amount of use of the glottal

236
00:11:02,880 --> 00:11:06,680
stop, but it's what shows up at the beginnings of words

237
00:11:06,680 --> 00:11:10,440
like, uh-uh, that catch that you're getting in your throat,

238
00:11:10,440 --> 00:11:13,160
uh-uh, meaning no, right?

239
00:11:13,160 --> 00:11:18,240
Or, uh-oh, meaning oh dear.

240
00:11:18,240 --> 00:11:19,840
That catch that you're getting in your throat,

241
00:11:19,840 --> 00:11:21,640
that's a glottal stop.

242
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:23,680
There are languages that are into glottal stops.

243
00:11:23,680 --> 00:11:25,120
They have lots of them.

244
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:27,600
English is not.

245
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:29,560
And then, so that's what, and the way you make that

246
00:11:29,560 --> 00:11:31,840
is by basically slamming your vocal folds together

247
00:11:31,840 --> 00:11:34,120
to close off the flow of air.

248
00:11:34,120 --> 00:11:36,440
You can also hold them kind of close together

249
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:38,240
and let the air whistle past.

250
00:11:38,240 --> 00:11:39,880
That's how you make an H, right?

251
00:11:39,880 --> 00:11:41,480
As in, what's my word up there?

252
00:11:41,480 --> 00:11:42,640
Help, yeah?

253
00:11:42,640 --> 00:11:45,200
So you're just slightly abrading the flow of air.

254
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:48,500
OK?

255
00:11:49,500 --> 00:11:51,940
Now, yeah, are there questions about any of that?

256
00:11:51,940 --> 00:11:53,980
So that was just a quick tour through the vocal tract.

257
00:11:53,980 --> 00:11:54,480
Yeah?

258
00:11:54,480 --> 00:11:56,460
Where is my cha?

259
00:11:56,460 --> 00:11:58,220
Oh, we haven't gotten to cha.

260
00:11:58,220 --> 00:11:59,900
Yeah, but good question.

261
00:11:59,900 --> 00:12:02,860
We can think of what that is.

262
00:12:02,860 --> 00:12:07,140
Cha, a cha sound at the beginning of a word like church.

263
00:12:07,140 --> 00:12:11,620
Also at the end of a word like church.

264
00:12:11,620 --> 00:12:13,780
Is a kind of dynamic sound, right?

265
00:12:13,980 --> 00:12:19,740
I think your tongue is in motion as you are making that sound.

266
00:12:19,740 --> 00:12:26,140
So it makes one sort of, it completely stops the flow there

267
00:12:26,140 --> 00:12:30,620
and then it gradually peels back and allows the air to flow out.

268
00:12:30,620 --> 00:12:32,540
You could think of that first thing

269
00:12:32,540 --> 00:12:36,220
it's doing as, I'm on completely the wrong slide,

270
00:12:36,220 --> 00:12:39,340
as being like an alveolar stop, right?

271
00:12:39,340 --> 00:12:42,660
So it's like a T, right?

272
00:12:43,180 --> 00:12:48,700
It's like a T. And then as it peels back,

273
00:12:48,700 --> 00:12:52,140
you end up with something like a cha, like a post alveolar.

274
00:12:52,140 --> 00:12:54,700
So it's a pretty complicated sound.

275
00:12:54,700 --> 00:12:55,580
And we'll talk about it.

276
00:12:55,580 --> 00:12:58,340
Yeah, that's a really good question.

277
00:12:58,340 --> 00:12:59,340
Are there questions?

278
00:12:59,340 --> 00:13:00,340
Somebody had a question?

279
00:13:00,340 --> 00:13:00,840
Yeah?

280
00:13:00,840 --> 00:13:04,020
What's the difference between an alveolar stop and an ending constant?

281
00:13:04,020 --> 00:13:05,020
I'm sorry, say it again.

282
00:13:05,020 --> 00:13:09,500
What's the difference between an alveolar stop and say an ending constant?

283
00:13:09,500 --> 00:13:11,700
An ending constant?

284
00:13:11,700 --> 00:13:14,940
What kind of ending constant did you get?

285
00:13:14,940 --> 00:13:17,300
Oh, oh, oh, yeah, so that's a really good point.

286
00:13:17,300 --> 00:13:23,900
OK, so a glottal stop, take a word like put, right?

287
00:13:27,140 --> 00:13:32,180
If you say put, your tongue, at least my tongue,

288
00:13:32,180 --> 00:13:33,420
touches the alveolar ridge.

289
00:13:33,420 --> 00:13:36,380
It goes where I said it would.

290
00:13:36,380 --> 00:13:37,660
But you're right.

291
00:13:37,660 --> 00:13:40,500
You also make a glottal stop, at least the way I just said it.

292
00:13:40,500 --> 00:13:41,780
Put, yeah.

293
00:13:46,780 --> 00:13:47,940
You could contrast that.

294
00:13:47,940 --> 00:13:50,860
If you didn't make the closure at the alveolar ridge,

295
00:13:50,860 --> 00:13:54,780
it would sound like put, right?

296
00:13:54,780 --> 00:13:56,900
Which is not an English word for me.

297
00:13:56,900 --> 00:13:58,860
There are dialects of English in which you would say,

298
00:13:58,860 --> 00:14:01,420
in which that's something you would say, right?

299
00:14:01,420 --> 00:14:03,700
Put.

300
00:14:03,700 --> 00:14:07,980
There are places in English where things

301
00:14:07,980 --> 00:14:11,780
that we write as other kinds of sounds actually

302
00:14:11,780 --> 00:14:13,980
are, in fact, glottal stops, at least in my English.

303
00:14:13,980 --> 00:14:19,900
So the difference for me between can and can't,

304
00:14:19,900 --> 00:14:22,060
my wife, who is Japanese, is driven crazy

305
00:14:22,060 --> 00:14:24,220
by the difference between can and can't.

306
00:14:24,220 --> 00:14:27,380
Because they're virtually the same, right?

307
00:14:27,380 --> 00:14:29,380
It's very hard for her to figure out often,

308
00:14:29,380 --> 00:14:31,140
whether I'm saying can or can't.

309
00:14:31,140 --> 00:14:34,860
Because the difference between them is really mostly just,

310
00:14:34,860 --> 00:14:39,060
can't is really just can plus a glottal stop.

311
00:14:39,060 --> 00:14:42,540
I'm not saying can't, usually, unless I'm being very emphatic.

312
00:14:42,540 --> 00:14:44,420
Yeah?

313
00:14:44,420 --> 00:14:45,020
Good question.

314
00:14:45,020 --> 00:14:46,020
Yes?

315
00:14:46,020 --> 00:14:49,140
It's a little bit difficult for the same sound

316
00:14:49,140 --> 00:14:54,540
to be heard, honestly, but with a different sound.

317
00:14:54,540 --> 00:14:55,220
Yes?

318
00:14:55,220 --> 00:14:56,820
Yes, there are things like that.

319
00:14:56,820 --> 00:14:58,460
Do you have something in mind?

320
00:14:58,460 --> 00:14:59,380
Oh, OK.

321
00:14:59,380 --> 00:15:03,380
So we haven't gotten yet to R. We'll get to R, eventually.

322
00:15:03,380 --> 00:15:06,180
But actually, people discovered at a certain point.

323
00:15:06,180 --> 00:15:09,460
So people investigate this kind of thing in all kinds of ways.

324
00:15:09,460 --> 00:15:11,020
One is the kind of thing we're all doing,

325
00:15:11,020 --> 00:15:13,300
where we just sort of sit and say, what is my mouth doing?

326
00:15:13,300 --> 00:15:15,300
Hm.

327
00:15:15,300 --> 00:15:19,860
There's other kinds of work where people, classically,

328
00:15:19,860 --> 00:15:23,340
paint the roof of your mouth with stuff that will come off.

329
00:15:23,340 --> 00:15:25,620
And then you have people produce a sound,

330
00:15:25,620 --> 00:15:28,100
and then you stick a camera in their mouth and take pictures

331
00:15:28,100 --> 00:15:31,540
and see which parts of the paint came off.

332
00:15:31,940 --> 00:15:34,460
You stick tubes down people's nose to measure airflow.

333
00:15:34,460 --> 00:15:37,500
You do all kinds of horrible, invasive things.

334
00:15:37,500 --> 00:15:40,020
These days, people do a lot of MRIs.

335
00:15:40,020 --> 00:15:45,980
I'm going to put on the website a couple of websites

336
00:15:45,980 --> 00:15:48,580
that have charts of all of the sounds

337
00:15:48,580 --> 00:15:50,860
that we're going to talk about plus many more,

338
00:15:50,860 --> 00:15:54,220
together with MRIs of the people's insides of people's mouths

339
00:15:54,220 --> 00:15:57,540
making these sounds so that you can sort of see the anatomy that's

340
00:15:57,540 --> 00:15:57,900
involved.

341
00:15:57,900 --> 00:15:59,220
You won't just have to think about it.

342
00:15:59,220 --> 00:16:00,460
One of the things people have discovered

343
00:16:00,460 --> 00:16:01,740
as they're doing this kind of work

344
00:16:01,740 --> 00:16:05,420
is that people just have different ways of producing R,

345
00:16:05,420 --> 00:16:07,820
that there are just different kinds of things

346
00:16:07,820 --> 00:16:10,580
you can do with your anatomy to make an R sound.

347
00:16:10,580 --> 00:16:12,740
And that's probably related to the fact

348
00:16:12,740 --> 00:16:14,820
that R is one of the kinds of sounds

349
00:16:14,820 --> 00:16:16,580
that people classically have trouble with.

350
00:16:16,580 --> 00:16:18,820
If you've been around small children, for example,

351
00:16:18,820 --> 00:16:22,260
it's kind of standard for them to not quite get R, right?

352
00:16:22,260 --> 00:16:24,940
And to say something that sounds more like a W

353
00:16:24,940 --> 00:16:27,220
at a certain stage.

354
00:16:27,220 --> 00:16:29,700
So I'm sorry, the short answer to your question is yes.

355
00:16:29,820 --> 00:16:31,100
And R might be an example.

356
00:16:33,820 --> 00:16:34,980
Yeah, good questions.

357
00:16:34,980 --> 00:16:38,100
Any other things people want to talk about?

358
00:16:38,100 --> 00:16:38,600
OK.

359
00:16:41,580 --> 00:16:44,500
So I went through that.

360
00:16:44,500 --> 00:16:46,740
So now I already sort of alluded to the fact

361
00:16:46,740 --> 00:16:52,900
that I had these slides that had various places

362
00:16:52,900 --> 00:16:53,940
of articulation on them.

363
00:16:53,940 --> 00:16:58,140
But of course, each slide had multiple sounds on it.

364
00:16:58,140 --> 00:17:00,420
And so the place of articulation is obviously

365
00:17:00,420 --> 00:17:01,860
not the whole story.

366
00:17:01,860 --> 00:17:03,220
Here's another part of the story.

367
00:17:03,220 --> 00:17:06,020
It's what's called voicing.

368
00:17:06,020 --> 00:17:10,220
So if you think about an S and a Z,

369
00:17:10,220 --> 00:17:11,820
those are both alveolar sounds.

370
00:17:11,820 --> 00:17:16,900
Your tongue is reaching toward the alveolar ridge.

371
00:17:16,900 --> 00:17:18,900
But they're not the same.

372
00:17:18,900 --> 00:17:20,980
What's the difference between them?

373
00:17:20,980 --> 00:17:22,900
If you think about, if you go back and forth between them,

374
00:17:23,380 --> 00:17:33,380
zzzz, zzzz, zzzz, zzzz, you can feel a buzzing.

375
00:17:33,380 --> 00:17:36,900
And if you put your hand right here, sorry,

376
00:17:36,900 --> 00:17:42,060
you put your hand right here, right here on your throat,

377
00:17:42,060 --> 00:17:45,220
then when you make the zzzz sound,

378
00:17:45,220 --> 00:17:48,300
you can sort of find the source of that buzzing.

379
00:17:48,300 --> 00:17:51,220
It's right there in your larynx.

380
00:17:51,220 --> 00:17:53,740
What's happening when you make a zzzz

381
00:17:53,740 --> 00:17:57,380
is that you're holding your vocal folds

382
00:17:57,380 --> 00:17:59,980
across the flow there in such a way

383
00:17:59,980 --> 00:18:02,900
that they will flap in the breeze as the air goes by.

384
00:18:02,900 --> 00:18:04,540
They'll vibrate.

385
00:18:04,540 --> 00:18:08,180
That's like whistling with a blade of grass,

386
00:18:08,180 --> 00:18:10,740
or playing a reed instrument.

387
00:18:10,740 --> 00:18:14,060
You're getting something to vibrate really fast.

388
00:18:14,060 --> 00:18:15,540
And that's what you're hearing.

389
00:18:15,540 --> 00:18:17,660
That's that buzzing sound that you're hearing and feeling

390
00:18:17,660 --> 00:18:21,740
if you put your hand right here when you're doing a zzzz.

391
00:18:21,740 --> 00:18:26,260
We say that zzzz is voiced and that szzz is voiceless.

392
00:18:26,260 --> 00:18:28,020
It's a distinction in voicing.

393
00:18:28,020 --> 00:18:28,520
Yes?

394
00:18:28,520 --> 00:18:31,460
Is the fact that it's not holding the throat

395
00:18:31,460 --> 00:18:32,820
in the breeze?

396
00:18:32,820 --> 00:18:35,580
Yeah, so the difference between cats and dogs?

397
00:18:35,580 --> 00:18:36,740
Yeah.

398
00:18:36,740 --> 00:18:39,220
So exactly, I was going to get to that later,

399
00:18:39,220 --> 00:18:40,620
but yes, that's exactly it.

400
00:18:40,620 --> 00:18:44,180
So what's the difference between a cat and a dog?

401
00:18:44,180 --> 00:18:46,300
Well, as far as a phenologist is concerned,

402
00:18:46,300 --> 00:18:51,060
the difference is that cat and dog end in sounds

403
00:18:51,060 --> 00:18:53,260
that differ in voicing.

404
00:18:53,260 --> 00:18:55,420
Is t voiced or voiceless?

405
00:18:55,420 --> 00:18:57,660
The t at the end of cat?

406
00:18:57,660 --> 00:18:58,900
Voiceless.

407
00:18:58,900 --> 00:19:01,580
And the g at the end of dog is voiced.

408
00:19:01,580 --> 00:19:03,220
And you're choosing s or z.

409
00:19:03,220 --> 00:19:05,660
You're putting the sound that agrees in voicing

410
00:19:05,660 --> 00:19:07,100
with the constant that's at the end.

411
00:19:07,100 --> 00:19:08,260
That's exactly right.

412
00:19:08,260 --> 00:19:08,760
Yeah?

413
00:19:08,760 --> 00:19:15,180
So then, is it possible to whist the zzzz?

414
00:19:15,220 --> 00:19:17,740
Yeah, so this is a really good question,

415
00:19:17,740 --> 00:19:19,860
which I was hoping not to get asked quite so soon,

416
00:19:19,860 --> 00:19:21,460
but no, that's good.

417
00:19:21,460 --> 00:19:24,420
OK, so she just asked, what are you

418
00:19:24,420 --> 00:19:25,860
doing when you're whispering?

419
00:19:25,860 --> 00:19:27,220
So if you think about what you're

420
00:19:27,220 --> 00:19:30,780
doing when you're whispering, first of all,

421
00:19:30,780 --> 00:19:33,700
your vocal cords are not vibrating at all.

422
00:19:33,700 --> 00:19:38,340
Your vocal cords are not vibrating at all at any point.

423
00:19:38,340 --> 00:19:42,020
So that should mean that you're not

424
00:19:42,020 --> 00:19:46,260
making the distinction between s and z, or between f and v.

425
00:19:46,260 --> 00:19:47,580
Or what's my other example here?

426
00:19:47,580 --> 00:19:48,860
The and the, right?

427
00:19:48,860 --> 00:19:51,700
So the difference between bath and bath

428
00:19:51,700 --> 00:19:56,780
is that the v is voiced and the th is voiceless.

429
00:19:56,780 --> 00:19:59,260
But that doesn't seem to be true, right?

430
00:19:59,260 --> 00:20:05,380
I mean, if you whisper safe and save,

431
00:20:05,380 --> 00:20:09,060
safe, save, you have the feeling

432
00:20:09,060 --> 00:20:11,740
that you can hear the difference between them, yeah?

433
00:20:11,780 --> 00:20:14,100
I think if you were to whisper one of these,

434
00:20:14,100 --> 00:20:16,700
so do a controlled experiment, go back to your dorm,

435
00:20:16,700 --> 00:20:18,460
whisper to your dorm mate, safe,

436
00:20:18,460 --> 00:20:21,260
and then find out what they think you said.

437
00:20:21,260 --> 00:20:27,340
Maybe warn them in advance, what you're going to do.

438
00:20:27,340 --> 00:20:32,020
I really, really, oh boy, the complaints.

439
00:20:32,020 --> 00:20:35,220
Yes, so there's got to be something else going on, right?

440
00:20:35,220 --> 00:20:36,500
Let's do this experiment again, though.

441
00:20:36,500 --> 00:20:38,620
So it's easiest for you, actually, with f and v.

442
00:20:38,620 --> 00:20:40,020
So let's do this thing again.

443
00:20:40,020 --> 00:20:45,740
If we go back and forth between f and v,

444
00:20:45,740 --> 00:20:47,700
which of them is voiced?

445
00:20:47,700 --> 00:20:48,540
The v, right?

446
00:20:48,540 --> 00:20:49,620
The v is voiced.

447
00:20:49,620 --> 00:20:51,500
OK, now do it again, but whisper.

448
00:20:58,780 --> 00:21:01,300
Does anybody feel a difference between f and v?

449
00:21:03,820 --> 00:21:07,100
I mean, not here, right?

450
00:21:07,100 --> 00:21:07,600
Yeah?

451
00:21:07,920 --> 00:21:13,080
So I, yes?

452
00:21:20,400 --> 00:21:21,240
Yeah, there might be a difference

453
00:21:21,240 --> 00:21:23,400
in the aperture of your mouth, I think you're right.

454
00:21:23,400 --> 00:21:25,360
And I think you're right, too, that there's

455
00:21:25,360 --> 00:21:28,840
a difference in how fast the air is flowing.

456
00:21:28,840 --> 00:21:30,720
For me, I actually have the opposite of your feeling.

457
00:21:30,720 --> 00:21:36,320
For me, for me, there's more air when there's an f,

458
00:21:36,320 --> 00:21:37,960
and less air when there's a v, I don't know.

459
00:21:37,960 --> 00:21:38,460
Yeah?

460
00:21:38,460 --> 00:21:41,600
It does need, but when I see v, it might look a little narrower.

461
00:21:41,600 --> 00:21:42,100
Ooh.

462
00:21:47,760 --> 00:21:50,840
Yeah, my jaw is moving as I do that.

463
00:21:50,840 --> 00:21:53,640
And I think that is affecting what my lips are doing.

464
00:21:53,640 --> 00:21:55,560
They're coming together more.

465
00:21:55,560 --> 00:21:57,320
So I think what's happening, maybe,

466
00:21:57,320 --> 00:22:01,480
maybe is an attempt to answer your question, is that,

467
00:22:01,480 --> 00:22:05,840
so I just said, sounds can be either voiced or voiceless.

468
00:22:05,840 --> 00:22:07,840
If they're voiced, what it means is that your vocal cords

469
00:22:07,840 --> 00:22:08,960
are vibrating.

470
00:22:08,960 --> 00:22:10,920
And I made it sound like the way you do that

471
00:22:10,920 --> 00:22:13,920
is, well, you stick your vocal cords into the flow of air,

472
00:22:13,920 --> 00:22:15,280
and you make them vibrate.

473
00:22:15,280 --> 00:22:16,720
But I think maybe what we're learning

474
00:22:16,720 --> 00:22:18,800
is that you do some other things, too,

475
00:22:18,800 --> 00:22:21,160
to optimize the flow of air so that you

476
00:22:21,160 --> 00:22:23,040
will get a good vibration going.

477
00:22:23,040 --> 00:22:25,400
That maybe if the flow of air is too fast,

478
00:22:25,400 --> 00:22:27,520
and maybe we're learning things about the aperture

479
00:22:27,520 --> 00:22:32,000
of your mouth as well, there's a way of making sure

480
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:33,960
that the pressure that you're getting,

481
00:22:33,960 --> 00:22:36,080
the sort of rush of air that you're getting on your vocal

482
00:22:36,080 --> 00:22:38,480
cords, will make them vibrate in just the right way.

483
00:22:38,480 --> 00:22:40,840
And you're manipulating all of that stuff

484
00:22:40,840 --> 00:22:42,640
without thinking about it.

485
00:22:42,640 --> 00:22:44,880
And you can still hear it when you whisper.

486
00:22:44,880 --> 00:22:47,440
So when you whisper, you're not engaging your vocal cords,

487
00:22:47,440 --> 00:22:48,960
but you're doing all the other stuff.

488
00:22:48,960 --> 00:22:51,960
And that's what you're using to hear the difference.

489
00:22:51,960 --> 00:22:53,640
There's experimental work on this,

490
00:22:53,640 --> 00:22:56,400
is the kind of thing people try to figure out.

491
00:22:56,400 --> 00:22:59,440
Yeah, really good question.

492
00:22:59,440 --> 00:23:00,440
OK.

493
00:23:00,440 --> 00:23:02,800
OK, so your vocal cords can either be vibrating

494
00:23:02,800 --> 00:23:03,920
or they cannot be vibrating.

495
00:23:04,680 --> 00:23:07,520
You have voiced sounds, and you have voiceless sounds.

496
00:23:07,520 --> 00:23:10,960
So S and Z and T and D are all alveolar,

497
00:23:10,960 --> 00:23:15,400
but S and T are voiceless, and Z and D are voiced.

498
00:23:15,400 --> 00:23:16,480
Does that all sound right?

499
00:23:16,480 --> 00:23:19,120
Is anyone upset by any of that?

500
00:23:19,120 --> 00:23:22,920
Disturbed, alarmed, hungry.

501
00:23:22,920 --> 00:23:23,640
Yeah, anything?

502
00:23:23,640 --> 00:23:24,800
OK, good.

503
00:23:24,800 --> 00:23:32,360
So back to the Polish plurals.

504
00:23:32,360 --> 00:23:36,760
So we saw before we convinced ourselves,

505
00:23:36,760 --> 00:23:38,720
or I convinced myself, and I tried

506
00:23:38,720 --> 00:23:41,280
to take the rest of you with me, kind of as collateral damage,

507
00:23:41,280 --> 00:23:45,680
that Polish has words that end in K

508
00:23:45,680 --> 00:23:48,320
and words that end in G underlyingly,

509
00:23:48,320 --> 00:23:50,880
but it also has a rule that changes G to K

510
00:23:50,880 --> 00:23:51,920
at the ends of words.

511
00:23:51,920 --> 00:23:55,240
That was Polish, right?

512
00:23:55,240 --> 00:24:00,200
OK, but it's not just G. So we can see some other pairs of words.

513
00:24:00,200 --> 00:24:01,960
I don't have any more minimal pairs for you,

514
00:24:01,960 --> 00:24:04,760
but you can see there's the same general tendency

515
00:24:04,760 --> 00:24:08,280
that if we look at singulars in Polish,

516
00:24:08,280 --> 00:24:13,080
that they can end in sounds like K or P or T or S.

517
00:24:13,080 --> 00:24:15,240
That's what we're seeing in these pairs.

518
00:24:15,240 --> 00:24:20,360
And that when you pluralize them, some nouns that end in P

519
00:24:20,360 --> 00:24:25,960
still end in P when you add the E, the suffix, like corpse,

520
00:24:25,960 --> 00:24:29,800
but some change the P to a B. And the same deal

521
00:24:29,800 --> 00:24:31,640
for these other ones.

522
00:24:31,640 --> 00:24:35,040
So what we're learning is it isn't just

523
00:24:35,040 --> 00:24:39,440
that G becomes K at the end of a word in Polish.

524
00:24:39,440 --> 00:24:41,720
There's this more general thing.

525
00:24:41,720 --> 00:24:43,160
What's the more general thing?

526
00:24:43,160 --> 00:24:45,280
What's going on here?

527
00:24:45,280 --> 00:24:47,800
What's the difference between G and K?

528
00:24:47,800 --> 00:24:48,800
So they're both velar.

529
00:24:51,280 --> 00:24:52,640
Yeah.

530
00:24:52,640 --> 00:24:55,360
Yeah, which one is voiced?

531
00:24:55,360 --> 00:24:59,280
The G. So what's happening is that the G, which was voiced,

532
00:24:59,280 --> 00:25:03,400
is becoming voiceless at the ends of words in Polish.

533
00:25:03,400 --> 00:25:05,400
What's happening with B and P?

534
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:08,560
Joseph?

535
00:25:08,560 --> 00:25:10,400
Voice A becomes voiceless.

536
00:25:10,400 --> 00:25:13,480
Yeah, the voiced B becomes the voiceless version, which is P.

537
00:25:13,480 --> 00:25:15,480
Those are both bilabial sounds.

538
00:25:15,480 --> 00:25:18,080
They involve both your lips.

539
00:25:18,080 --> 00:25:18,580
Yes?

540
00:25:18,580 --> 00:25:19,800
How is D voiced?

541
00:25:19,800 --> 00:25:20,280
Sorry?

542
00:25:20,280 --> 00:25:21,240
How is D voiced?

543
00:25:21,240 --> 00:25:23,840
How is D voiced?

544
00:25:23,840 --> 00:25:25,000
Oh, B.

545
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:25,840
B.

546
00:25:26,560 --> 00:25:31,320
B. You're raising a good point.

547
00:25:31,320 --> 00:25:34,600
There's a reason that I started with sounds like S and Z

548
00:25:34,600 --> 00:25:42,120
and F and V, because you can go for as long as you have breath.

549
00:25:42,120 --> 00:25:46,080
Whereas B, there's a limit to how long you can be.

550
00:25:49,120 --> 00:25:51,800
We just said the way voicing works

551
00:25:51,800 --> 00:25:54,840
is that you've got air flowing across your vocal folds

552
00:25:54,840 --> 00:25:56,840
and making them vibrate.

553
00:25:56,840 --> 00:25:59,320
And for a Z, you can see how that would work.

554
00:25:59,320 --> 00:26:00,760
So the air just flows.

555
00:26:00,760 --> 00:26:06,640
For a B, well, the air only has so far to go.

556
00:26:06,640 --> 00:26:09,720
That's one reason you can't keep a B going for very long.

557
00:26:09,720 --> 00:26:11,320
All that air has to get in.

558
00:26:11,320 --> 00:26:13,040
It has to flow past your vocal folds

559
00:26:13,040 --> 00:26:14,640
to get them to vibrate a little bit.

560
00:26:14,640 --> 00:26:17,840
And then it gets to your mouth, and then it has to stop.

561
00:26:17,840 --> 00:26:19,840
There are other departments that are better at this than I am,

562
00:26:19,840 --> 00:26:21,840
but the air pressure in your mouth

563
00:26:21,840 --> 00:26:23,560
is going to build up past a certain point.

564
00:26:23,560 --> 00:26:26,280
You won't be able to keep doing that.

565
00:26:26,280 --> 00:26:28,080
But you do it for as long as you can.

566
00:26:28,080 --> 00:26:30,400
That's the sense of much it's voiced.

567
00:26:30,400 --> 00:26:32,240
Yeah, good question.

568
00:26:32,240 --> 00:26:33,560
Yeah?

569
00:26:33,560 --> 00:26:35,240
OK.

570
00:26:35,240 --> 00:26:38,760
So yeah, what's happening in Polish is not just G becomes K.

571
00:26:38,760 --> 00:26:43,160
It's voiced sounds are becoming voiceless.

572
00:26:43,160 --> 00:26:49,080
So Z becomes S, D becomes T, B becomes P, and G becomes K.

573
00:26:49,080 --> 00:26:51,600
So it's sometimes called final devoicing.

574
00:26:51,600 --> 00:26:54,320
And it's a cross-linguistically quite common phenomenon.

575
00:26:57,320 --> 00:26:58,920
OK.

576
00:26:58,920 --> 00:27:02,920
So yeah.

577
00:27:02,920 --> 00:27:04,240
All right.

578
00:27:04,240 --> 00:27:07,160
So we've talked about place of articulation,

579
00:27:07,160 --> 00:27:09,280
and we've talked about voicing.

580
00:27:09,280 --> 00:27:11,840
Now we need to talk about another dimension

581
00:27:11,840 --> 00:27:14,440
for categorizing sounds, which is called

582
00:27:14,440 --> 00:27:16,080
manner of articulation.

583
00:27:16,080 --> 00:27:19,800
So think about S and T. They're both alveolar

584
00:27:19,840 --> 00:27:22,240
and they're both voiceless.

585
00:27:22,240 --> 00:27:24,560
But they're different from each other.

586
00:27:24,560 --> 00:27:26,760
And the way we distinguish them is via what

587
00:27:26,760 --> 00:27:28,680
we call manner of articulation.

588
00:27:28,680 --> 00:27:31,640
So they're both voiceless alveolar sounds.

589
00:27:31,640 --> 00:27:37,440
But T is a stop, and S is what's called a fricative.

590
00:27:37,440 --> 00:27:41,400
So stops are also called plosives.

591
00:27:41,400 --> 00:27:44,640
I promise never, ever to call them plosives.

592
00:27:44,640 --> 00:27:46,880
I will always call them stops, because that's

593
00:27:46,880 --> 00:27:48,160
what I grew up calling them.

594
00:27:48,160 --> 00:27:50,000
But you will sometimes see things written in which

595
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:51,960
they're called plosives.

596
00:27:51,960 --> 00:27:54,080
In homeworks, if you ever need to write about them,

597
00:27:54,080 --> 00:27:55,560
feel free to call them either one.

598
00:27:55,560 --> 00:27:57,320
Doesn't matter.

599
00:27:57,320 --> 00:27:59,280
I like calling them stops, because they're

600
00:27:59,280 --> 00:28:02,800
named after the fact that, well, they stop the flow there.

601
00:28:02,800 --> 00:28:03,520
That's what they do.

602
00:28:03,520 --> 00:28:04,760
That's what a stop is.

603
00:28:04,760 --> 00:28:09,240
So the error is coming out of your lungs, and it gets stopped.

604
00:28:09,240 --> 00:28:13,160
Fricatives, like S, are sounds in which you don't stop the flow

605
00:28:13,160 --> 00:28:17,560
there, but you narrow some aperture

606
00:28:17,560 --> 00:28:20,720
enough to create turbulent airflow, which you hear

607
00:28:20,720 --> 00:28:22,880
as a kind of hissing sound.

608
00:28:22,880 --> 00:28:26,520
So sounds like S, and sh, and f, and f.

609
00:28:26,520 --> 00:28:27,480
These are all fricatives.

610
00:28:34,480 --> 00:28:37,320
So for T and D, the airflow is stopped.

611
00:28:37,320 --> 00:28:41,240
For S and Z, the airflow is restricted, but is not stopped.

612
00:28:41,240 --> 00:28:45,040
So you kind of hold your tongue close to the alveolar ridge,

613
00:28:45,040 --> 00:28:48,840
but you allow air to keep flowing through.

614
00:28:48,840 --> 00:28:51,160
That's why, since the conversation we just had,

615
00:28:51,160 --> 00:28:54,080
that's why an S, you can keep going

616
00:28:54,080 --> 00:28:56,160
for as long as you have breath in your lungs,

617
00:28:56,160 --> 00:28:58,920
whereas a T, you can't keep executing it.

618
00:29:03,840 --> 00:29:08,160
So now we have these three ways of categorizing

619
00:29:08,160 --> 00:29:10,760
these kinds of speech sounds.

620
00:29:10,760 --> 00:29:13,360
Place and manner and voicing.

621
00:29:13,400 --> 00:29:17,120
Place of articulation, manner of articulation, and voicing.

622
00:29:17,120 --> 00:29:19,360
So there are a bunch of places of articulation

623
00:29:19,360 --> 00:29:21,680
over there on the left.

624
00:29:21,680 --> 00:29:24,320
And the sounds that we have mainly talked about

625
00:29:24,320 --> 00:29:26,520
have been either stops or fricatives.

626
00:29:26,520 --> 00:29:32,000
And then each of these places on the table,

627
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:34,800
there's a pair of sounds.

628
00:29:34,800 --> 00:29:37,080
And I hope I managed to do this right.

629
00:29:37,080 --> 00:29:39,160
Yes, it looks like I did.

630
00:29:39,160 --> 00:29:42,840
In all of these pairs, you've got both a voiced

631
00:29:42,840 --> 00:29:43,880
and a voiceless sound.

632
00:29:43,880 --> 00:29:46,880
Which one is first?

633
00:29:46,880 --> 00:29:48,080
The voiced sound.

634
00:29:48,080 --> 00:29:50,640
So in all of those, there's a pair.

635
00:29:50,640 --> 00:29:52,800
So you've got zh and sh, for example,

636
00:29:52,800 --> 00:29:54,360
the post-alveolar fricatives.

637
00:29:54,360 --> 00:29:56,000
And the zh is the voiced one.

638
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:58,120
And the sh is the voiceless one.

639
00:29:58,120 --> 00:30:00,120
Yeah?

640
00:30:00,120 --> 00:30:00,680
OK, good.

641
00:30:03,640 --> 00:30:04,120
All right.

642
00:30:04,120 --> 00:30:06,360
So now, new class of sounds.

643
00:30:06,360 --> 00:30:08,440
This is a new manner of articulation.

644
00:30:08,440 --> 00:30:11,360
So we've got d, which is a voiced alveolar stop,

645
00:30:11,360 --> 00:30:14,400
and zh, which is a voiced alveolar fricative.

646
00:30:14,400 --> 00:30:17,040
And now we need to think about n.

647
00:30:17,040 --> 00:30:25,240
Well, n is voiced, and it's a stop in the sense

648
00:30:25,240 --> 00:30:28,720
that you are stopping the air from flowing through your mouth.

649
00:30:28,720 --> 00:30:30,800
If you think about what's happening inside your mouth

650
00:30:30,800 --> 00:30:33,360
during an n, your tongue is jammed

651
00:30:33,360 --> 00:30:38,480
against your alveolar ridge, just as it would be for a t.

652
00:30:38,480 --> 00:30:43,640
So I love the sound of phonetics in the morning.

653
00:30:43,640 --> 00:30:46,440
It's great.

654
00:30:46,440 --> 00:30:49,520
So when you're doing an n, you've

655
00:30:49,520 --> 00:30:50,840
stopped the flow of air in your mouth,

656
00:30:50,840 --> 00:30:53,200
and so n is technically a stop.

657
00:30:53,200 --> 00:30:55,160
It's called a stop because there's no air coming out

658
00:30:55,160 --> 00:30:55,800
of your mouth.

659
00:30:55,800 --> 00:30:57,680
But there's air coming out somewhere.

660
00:30:57,680 --> 00:31:00,720
It's coming out of your nose.

661
00:31:00,720 --> 00:31:04,360
And you can tell because if you hold your nose,

662
00:31:04,360 --> 00:31:06,680
you will not be able to make an n.

663
00:31:06,680 --> 00:31:11,000
So the reason that you can keep an n going again

664
00:31:11,000 --> 00:31:13,960
for as long as you have breath is, well,

665
00:31:13,960 --> 00:31:15,320
the air has some place to go.

666
00:31:15,320 --> 00:31:18,160
It's going out your nose.

667
00:31:18,160 --> 00:31:20,120
What's happening when you make an n

668
00:31:20,120 --> 00:31:24,400
is that you are lowering the velum, which

669
00:31:24,400 --> 00:31:26,960
is this doohickey in the back of your mouth

670
00:31:26,960 --> 00:31:31,000
that partitions your nose from your mouth.

671
00:31:31,000 --> 00:31:34,240
By lowering it, you're allowing air to flow through your nose.

672
00:31:34,240 --> 00:31:38,320
So for t and d, the airflow is stopped at the alveolar ridge.

673
00:31:38,320 --> 00:31:41,840
For n, the airflow is also stopped at the alveolar ridge.

674
00:31:41,840 --> 00:31:43,280
It can't go through the mouth.

675
00:31:43,280 --> 00:31:46,960
It's stopped right there, but it goes through your nose.

676
00:31:46,960 --> 00:31:51,360
This is a nasal stop, a voiced alveolar nasal.

677
00:31:51,360 --> 00:31:54,160
People often just call them nasals

678
00:31:54,160 --> 00:31:58,040
because nasal fricatives are messy.

679
00:31:58,040 --> 00:31:59,600
Actually, not possible, possibly.

680
00:32:00,400 --> 00:32:00,900
OK?

681
00:32:04,000 --> 00:32:06,080
So let me go back.

682
00:32:06,080 --> 00:32:09,280
n is an alveolar nasal.

683
00:32:09,280 --> 00:32:11,080
What would a bilabial nasal sound like?

684
00:32:15,680 --> 00:32:18,480
Close your lips and allow the air to flow through your nose.

685
00:32:18,480 --> 00:32:19,640
And a velar nasal?

686
00:32:24,320 --> 00:32:27,160
Remember that velar means the place of articulation

687
00:32:27,160 --> 00:32:28,720
for a k or a g.

688
00:32:28,720 --> 00:32:31,400
So you make a k or a g sound, and then just

689
00:32:31,400 --> 00:32:33,120
let air flow through your nose.

690
00:32:33,120 --> 00:32:37,920
That's the sound at the end of a word like song, or king,

691
00:32:37,920 --> 00:32:40,040
or making a velar nasal.

692
00:32:40,040 --> 00:32:43,560
English doesn't allow words to begin with velar nasals,

693
00:32:43,560 --> 00:32:45,600
but there are languages that do.

694
00:32:45,600 --> 00:32:50,200
So Tagalog, for example, the word for now, right now,

695
00:32:50,200 --> 00:32:51,960
is ngayun.

696
00:32:51,960 --> 00:32:53,880
So it starts with a velar nasal.

697
00:32:53,880 --> 00:32:55,920
One of the entertaining things about learning Tagalog

698
00:32:55,920 --> 00:32:58,480
is learning how to make sounds that start with velar nasals.

699
00:32:58,520 --> 00:33:00,640
You're an English speaker, you're not used to it.

700
00:33:00,640 --> 00:33:01,640
Yeah?

701
00:33:05,640 --> 00:33:08,560
OK, so there's the table again.

702
00:33:08,560 --> 00:33:10,640
So we've got stops and fricatives and nasals.

703
00:33:10,640 --> 00:33:12,840
Nasal stops.

704
00:33:12,840 --> 00:33:14,680
Things can be voiced or voiceless.

705
00:33:14,680 --> 00:33:17,000
And we've got all those places of articulation

706
00:33:17,000 --> 00:33:18,040
over there on the left.

707
00:33:18,040 --> 00:33:19,480
Are there questions about any of this?

708
00:33:19,480 --> 00:33:21,240
Is anybody looking at this and saying, whoa,

709
00:33:21,240 --> 00:33:24,680
this table has grown out of my ability to keep a word?

710
00:33:24,680 --> 00:33:25,180
Yeah?

711
00:33:25,180 --> 00:33:25,680
Yeah.

712
00:33:25,680 --> 00:33:26,180
Yeah?

713
00:33:26,180 --> 00:33:27,560
What English doesn't allow?

714
00:33:27,600 --> 00:33:30,680
English doesn't allow words to begin with velar nasals.

715
00:33:30,680 --> 00:33:32,600
We don't have any words that start with the sound

716
00:33:32,600 --> 00:33:35,200
that's at the end of words like song.

717
00:33:35,200 --> 00:33:38,800
We don't have words like ngayun.

718
00:33:38,800 --> 00:33:40,960
English doesn't have words like that.

719
00:33:40,960 --> 00:33:43,120
There are plenty of languages that do.

720
00:33:43,120 --> 00:33:44,200
Tagalog is one.

721
00:33:44,200 --> 00:33:45,640
Cantonese is one.

722
00:33:45,640 --> 00:33:46,640
There are a bunch of others.

723
00:33:46,640 --> 00:33:49,360
But English doesn't have that.

724
00:33:49,360 --> 00:33:50,840
Yes?

725
00:33:50,960 --> 00:33:55,640
I noticed there's not a j in practice or a y.

726
00:33:55,640 --> 00:33:58,200
It's called paladol.

727
00:33:58,200 --> 00:34:00,680
Oh, yeah.

728
00:34:00,680 --> 00:34:05,200
Actually, I don't actually know where this j would go.

729
00:34:05,200 --> 00:34:06,040
Yeah.

730
00:34:06,040 --> 00:34:08,720
So we haven't yet gotten to the kind of sound

731
00:34:08,720 --> 00:34:11,720
that that j, the sound at the beginning of the year.

732
00:34:11,720 --> 00:34:12,600
You're absolutely right.

733
00:34:12,600 --> 00:34:14,360
There's a gap there.

734
00:34:14,360 --> 00:34:17,360
And I've put the paladol line there partly

735
00:34:17,360 --> 00:34:19,920
to get you to ask exactly that kind of question.

736
00:34:19,920 --> 00:34:22,720
So one of the points of this table,

737
00:34:22,720 --> 00:34:24,880
it's kind of like when the periodic table was first

738
00:34:24,880 --> 00:34:25,920
invented, I guess.

739
00:34:25,920 --> 00:34:28,560
So we have this system for categorizing sounds.

740
00:34:28,560 --> 00:34:30,480
And now we get to look at it and say, well, wait, why

741
00:34:30,480 --> 00:34:32,520
isn't there anything there or there?

742
00:34:32,520 --> 00:34:34,720
And what would it be like if it were there?

743
00:34:34,720 --> 00:34:37,360
We'll do a little bit of that in a second.

744
00:34:37,360 --> 00:34:38,880
But yeah, you're right.

745
00:34:38,880 --> 00:34:42,080
So we haven't yet talked about the kind of sound

746
00:34:42,080 --> 00:34:45,720
that the IPA symbol j, the sound we usually

747
00:34:45,720 --> 00:34:48,480
write with y in English, the sound at the beginning of the year,

748
00:34:48,480 --> 00:34:50,360
we haven't put that on the table yet.

749
00:34:50,360 --> 00:34:51,160
You're right.

750
00:34:51,160 --> 00:34:52,840
It's not a stop.

751
00:34:52,840 --> 00:34:55,080
It's also not a fricative because you're not making

752
00:34:55,080 --> 00:34:56,880
any turbulence in the airflow.

753
00:34:56,880 --> 00:34:58,040
And it's not nasal.

754
00:34:58,040 --> 00:34:59,240
So it's another kind of thing.

755
00:34:59,240 --> 00:35:01,280
We'll get to it.

756
00:35:01,280 --> 00:35:01,840
Good question.

757
00:35:01,840 --> 00:35:03,760
Other questions?

758
00:35:03,760 --> 00:35:05,560
This?

759
00:35:05,560 --> 00:35:07,280
OK.

760
00:35:07,280 --> 00:35:09,280
All right.

761
00:35:09,280 --> 00:35:10,400
So yeah, OK.

762
00:35:10,400 --> 00:35:12,640
So this way of classifying the sounds

763
00:35:12,640 --> 00:35:13,720
leads us to wonder about gaps.

764
00:35:13,720 --> 00:35:14,960
This is, yes, thanks.

765
00:35:14,960 --> 00:35:16,480
Norvin, yeah, you set that up nicely.

766
00:35:19,640 --> 00:35:22,160
So let's think about some of these gaps.

767
00:35:22,160 --> 00:35:26,320
So English, for example, has bilabial stops, P and B.

768
00:35:26,320 --> 00:35:29,280
And it has a bilabial nasal and M. But it

769
00:35:29,280 --> 00:35:32,400
doesn't have a bilabial fricative.

770
00:35:32,400 --> 00:35:34,080
What would a bilabial fricative sound like?

771
00:35:37,360 --> 00:35:40,360
It would sound kind of like blowing out a candle.

772
00:35:40,360 --> 00:35:42,760
Open your lips just enough to let some air come out and then

773
00:35:42,760 --> 00:35:44,280
blow.

774
00:35:44,280 --> 00:35:45,440
That's a bilabial trill.

775
00:35:45,440 --> 00:35:48,680
We will get to that.

776
00:35:48,680 --> 00:35:51,920
Either that or you were just having fun, I'm not sure.

777
00:35:51,920 --> 00:35:55,240
There are languages that have that.

778
00:35:55,240 --> 00:35:56,800
So but a bilabial fricative, English

779
00:35:56,800 --> 00:35:58,360
doesn't have a bilabial fricative.

780
00:35:58,360 --> 00:35:59,960
But there are languages that do.

781
00:35:59,960 --> 00:36:01,240
Japanese does, for example.

782
00:36:01,240 --> 00:36:03,480
So in Japanese, the sound that when

783
00:36:03,480 --> 00:36:05,800
people are writing Japanese in the Roman alphabet,

784
00:36:05,800 --> 00:36:08,040
when they write an F, like when they

785
00:36:08,040 --> 00:36:11,600
write the name of this mountain, they'll write it with an F.

786
00:36:11,600 --> 00:36:13,280
But in Japanese, that's not an F.

787
00:36:13,280 --> 00:36:14,440
It's a bilabial fricative.

788
00:36:14,440 --> 00:36:16,320
It's Fuji, Fuji.

789
00:36:16,320 --> 00:36:19,360
If you learn Japanese, you must learn to pronounce

790
00:36:19,360 --> 00:36:23,280
the F bilabially rather than labiodentally.

791
00:36:23,280 --> 00:36:25,600
So in English, we have a labiodental F

792
00:36:25,600 --> 00:36:28,960
with our lower lip against our teeth.

793
00:36:28,960 --> 00:36:31,400
In Japanese, F, your teeth are not involved.

794
00:36:31,400 --> 00:36:32,280
It is only your lips.

795
00:36:36,760 --> 00:36:39,600
What would a bilabial fricative sound like?

796
00:36:39,600 --> 00:36:42,920
Is this a voice or a voiceless bilabial fricative?

797
00:36:42,920 --> 00:36:44,480
Voiceless.

798
00:36:44,480 --> 00:36:46,640
What would it sound like if it were voiced?

799
00:36:46,640 --> 00:36:48,640
Vrr.

800
00:36:48,640 --> 00:36:50,320
And that exists, too.

801
00:36:50,320 --> 00:36:51,840
There are dialects of Spanish, for example,

802
00:36:51,840 --> 00:36:54,240
that have that between vowels.

803
00:36:54,240 --> 00:36:59,560
If you have the letter B between vowels in words like aruela,

804
00:36:59,560 --> 00:37:06,320
that B has that sound, a voiced bilabial fricative.

805
00:37:06,320 --> 00:37:09,480
Moving across the chart, I've got a nasal stop there.

806
00:37:09,480 --> 00:37:11,240
Is that nasal voiced or voiceless?

807
00:37:13,440 --> 00:37:15,600
Voiced.

808
00:37:15,600 --> 00:37:18,800
What would it sound like if it were voiceless?

809
00:37:18,800 --> 00:37:19,520
I should step back.

810
00:37:23,960 --> 00:37:25,640
English doesn't have that.

811
00:37:25,640 --> 00:37:28,760
But there are languages out there that do.

812
00:37:28,760 --> 00:37:31,760
Languages like Hmong, for example,

813
00:37:31,760 --> 00:37:34,000
which is a minority language spoken in,

814
00:37:34,000 --> 00:37:36,160
actually it's quite a large minority language spoken

815
00:37:36,160 --> 00:37:38,200
in Vietnam.

816
00:37:38,200 --> 00:37:42,200
Hmong has that kind of sound.

817
00:37:42,200 --> 00:37:45,880
Tibetan has that kind of sound, too.

818
00:37:45,880 --> 00:37:47,080
OK.

819
00:37:47,080 --> 00:37:50,120
Let's skip palatal and do velar.

820
00:37:50,120 --> 00:37:53,800
We've got velar stops in English, K and G, and G.

821
00:37:53,800 --> 00:37:56,280
And we've got velar nasals.

822
00:37:56,280 --> 00:38:00,720
What would a velar fricative sound like?

823
00:38:00,720 --> 00:38:01,520
Yes.

824
00:38:01,520 --> 00:38:03,880
She's alerting me that she's not just hissing at me.

825
00:38:03,880 --> 00:38:04,960
She's making a velar fricative.

826
00:38:04,960 --> 00:38:07,960
Yes.

827
00:38:07,960 --> 00:38:11,520
Is that a voiced or a voiceless velar fricative?

828
00:38:11,520 --> 00:38:13,960
Voiceless, yeah.

829
00:38:13,960 --> 00:38:15,520
English doesn't have that.

830
00:38:15,520 --> 00:38:17,360
But there are languages that do.

831
00:38:17,360 --> 00:38:19,800
Languages like German say that's one of the sounds

832
00:38:19,800 --> 00:38:22,960
that they write with the letters C and H. At the end of composers

833
00:38:22,960 --> 00:38:27,360
names like Bach, Johann Sebastian Bach.

834
00:38:27,360 --> 00:38:29,760
His name ends with a velar fricative.

835
00:38:29,760 --> 00:38:33,240
Russian has this sound.

836
00:38:33,240 --> 00:38:37,520
Shows up in the names of authors like Chekhov.

837
00:38:37,520 --> 00:38:39,400
Sound.

838
00:38:39,400 --> 00:38:42,280
What would a velar fricative sound like if it were voiced?

839
00:38:48,120 --> 00:38:50,400
And English doesn't have that either.

840
00:38:50,400 --> 00:38:52,360
But their languages that do, again,

841
00:38:52,360 --> 00:38:53,600
they're dialects of Spanish, where

842
00:38:53,600 --> 00:38:55,720
if you have a G between vowels, it'll

843
00:38:55,720 --> 00:38:57,640
get this kind of sound in words like agua.

844
00:39:01,920 --> 00:39:03,840
So we've talked about various kinds of nasals.

845
00:39:03,840 --> 00:39:04,720
Nasal stops.

846
00:39:04,720 --> 00:39:08,720
So an M is a bilabial nasal, right?

847
00:39:08,720 --> 00:39:11,440
Where you close the flow there at your lips

848
00:39:11,440 --> 00:39:14,600
and allow the air to flow through your nasal cavity

849
00:39:14,600 --> 00:39:15,560
by lowering your velums.

850
00:39:15,560 --> 00:39:19,360
You get an N. Or you can stop the flow there in other places.

851
00:39:19,360 --> 00:39:20,880
You can have an alveolar nasal, right?

852
00:39:20,880 --> 00:39:23,880
N, you can have a velar nasal.

853
00:39:23,880 --> 00:39:25,560
What would a glottal nasal sound like?

854
00:39:30,640 --> 00:39:34,080
Trick question, you would need surgery.

855
00:39:34,080 --> 00:39:38,320
So the way nasal stops work is you're

856
00:39:38,320 --> 00:39:41,400
stopping the flow of air somewhere in your vocal tract,

857
00:39:41,400 --> 00:39:45,000
but you're allowing the air to flow through your nasal cavity.

858
00:39:45,000 --> 00:39:46,840
That's what a nasal stop is.

859
00:39:46,840 --> 00:39:50,280
Here, let's get back to one of these sedgital sections.

860
00:39:50,280 --> 00:39:53,520
So that N there, right?

861
00:39:53,520 --> 00:39:55,800
What you're doing is you're stopping the flow of air

862
00:39:55,800 --> 00:39:57,800
there at the alveolar ridge, right?

863
00:39:57,800 --> 00:39:59,960
But you're lowering the velum to let the air flow through.

864
00:39:59,960 --> 00:40:03,360
Or an M, you'd be closing the flow of air at your lips

865
00:40:03,400 --> 00:40:05,560
and lowering your velum to let the air flow through.

866
00:40:05,560 --> 00:40:09,720
Or a velar nasal, a N, like at the end of King.

867
00:40:09,720 --> 00:40:11,480
You're making a closure at the velum,

868
00:40:11,480 --> 00:40:13,160
but you're also lowering the velum and letting

869
00:40:13,160 --> 00:40:14,520
the air flow through.

870
00:40:14,520 --> 00:40:18,520
A glottal nasal, you'd have to stop the flow of air down

871
00:40:18,520 --> 00:40:26,120
there at the vocal folds and let the air go through your velum.

872
00:40:26,120 --> 00:40:27,720
But if you're stopping it down there,

873
00:40:27,720 --> 00:40:29,880
it can't be going through your velum.

874
00:40:29,880 --> 00:40:32,040
So you would need, again, as I say,

875
00:40:32,040 --> 00:40:34,880
probably there are unethical surgeons who would modify you

876
00:40:34,880 --> 00:40:36,480
so that you could make glottal nasals.

877
00:40:36,480 --> 00:40:40,240
You need extra ways to get air to flow through your nasal cavity.

878
00:40:40,240 --> 00:40:41,920
I'm not recommending this, by the way.

879
00:40:45,040 --> 00:40:48,040
All right.

880
00:40:48,040 --> 00:40:51,040
Cool.

881
00:40:51,040 --> 00:40:53,920
So those are some of the gaps.

882
00:40:53,920 --> 00:40:56,800
Oh, and I flagged a gap that I didn't talk about.

883
00:40:56,800 --> 00:40:58,920
Sorry, a couple of gaps.

884
00:40:58,920 --> 00:41:03,160
So English has interdental fricatives, th and the.

885
00:41:03,160 --> 00:41:05,240
And as I said, those are cross-linguistically kind

886
00:41:05,240 --> 00:41:08,800
of rare, which is why if you're trying

887
00:41:08,800 --> 00:41:11,280
to do an accent from various kinds of places,

888
00:41:11,280 --> 00:41:13,840
for example, in Europe, one of the things you do

889
00:41:13,840 --> 00:41:16,760
is replace your TH sounds with other kinds of sounds.

890
00:41:16,760 --> 00:41:18,760
So if you're doing a French accent,

891
00:41:18,760 --> 00:41:22,440
you replace your TH sounds with Zs.

892
00:41:22,440 --> 00:41:23,880
Or if you're doing a German accent,

893
00:41:23,880 --> 00:41:27,040
you replace your TH sounds with Ts.

894
00:41:27,040 --> 00:41:29,520
English has interdental fricatives,

895
00:41:29,520 --> 00:41:34,480
and it has alveolar stops, T and D. There are languages out there

896
00:41:34,480 --> 00:41:37,400
that have what are called dental stops.

897
00:41:37,400 --> 00:41:39,720
So a dental stop, so the alveolar stop, again,

898
00:41:39,720 --> 00:41:41,960
involves your tongue touching the alveolar ridge

899
00:41:41,960 --> 00:41:43,600
and stopping the flow there, there.

900
00:41:43,600 --> 00:41:46,480
For a dental stop, your tongue is touching your teeth.

901
00:41:46,480 --> 00:41:48,440
So you're not saying atah.

902
00:41:48,440 --> 00:41:52,360
You're saying atah, atah, atah.

903
00:41:52,360 --> 00:41:54,960
English does not have those, but there are languages out there

904
00:41:54,960 --> 00:41:56,200
that do.

905
00:41:56,200 --> 00:41:58,080
If you're studying another language,

906
00:41:58,080 --> 00:41:59,800
this is a kind of thing to think about,

907
00:41:59,800 --> 00:42:01,400
because sometimes your teacher will not

908
00:42:01,400 --> 00:42:02,920
be thinking about this.

909
00:42:02,920 --> 00:42:04,200
But you should be asking yourself,

910
00:42:04,200 --> 00:42:07,520
is this sound that sounds sort of like a T?

911
00:42:07,520 --> 00:42:10,360
Is it an alveolar T or is it a dental T?

912
00:42:10,360 --> 00:42:11,680
Part of your job, if you're learning

913
00:42:11,680 --> 00:42:13,400
ta-da-log, for example, is to learn

914
00:42:13,400 --> 00:42:15,760
to make dental Ts instead of alveolar Ts,

915
00:42:15,760 --> 00:42:18,240
because that's what they've got.

916
00:42:18,240 --> 00:42:20,240
Yeah?

917
00:42:20,240 --> 00:42:23,400
If you're thinking about, like, with your teeth,

918
00:42:24,160 --> 00:42:27,040
what say someone doesn't have teeth?

919
00:42:27,040 --> 00:42:30,040
Yes, yeah?

920
00:42:30,040 --> 00:42:31,280
How about that?

921
00:42:31,280 --> 00:42:32,160
Well.

922
00:42:32,160 --> 00:42:35,240
Would it be like a gesture?

923
00:42:35,240 --> 00:42:38,160
I think, I believe that is what people do, yeah.

924
00:42:38,160 --> 00:42:41,000
So you make a closure.

925
00:42:41,000 --> 00:42:43,000
We can talk about interdental fricatives, right?

926
00:42:43,000 --> 00:42:46,600
So somebody who doesn't have teeth and wants to do a th,

927
00:42:46,600 --> 00:42:47,400
what do they do?

928
00:42:47,400 --> 00:42:51,560
I think their job is to create turbulent airflow

929
00:42:51,560 --> 00:42:54,040
with their tongue between where their teeth would

930
00:42:54,040 --> 00:42:56,920
be if they only had teeth, right?

931
00:42:56,920 --> 00:42:58,800
And I think there's something similar going on

932
00:42:58,800 --> 00:42:59,840
with dental stops.

933
00:42:59,840 --> 00:43:01,240
But this is exactly the kind of thing

934
00:43:01,240 --> 00:43:03,120
that experimental phoneticists do,

935
00:43:03,120 --> 00:43:05,040
is to try to figure out one of the things that,

936
00:43:05,040 --> 00:43:07,160
they do many things, some of them kind of creepy,

937
00:43:07,160 --> 00:43:09,720
to try to figure out what's going on in your vocal tract

938
00:43:09,720 --> 00:43:12,520
exactly as you do this stuff.

939
00:43:12,520 --> 00:43:15,040
There's all this work on what people

940
00:43:15,040 --> 00:43:17,920
do to compensate for various kinds of obstructions

941
00:43:17,920 --> 00:43:18,680
in the vocal tract.

942
00:43:18,680 --> 00:43:20,640
So you have people bite on a block.

943
00:43:20,640 --> 00:43:23,200
And you put something solid in their mouth.

944
00:43:23,200 --> 00:43:24,680
And then you're like, OK, so now what

945
00:43:24,680 --> 00:43:28,560
will you do with your tongue to make the sounds?

946
00:43:28,560 --> 00:43:29,520
As best you can.

947
00:43:29,520 --> 00:43:31,800
All kinds of weird stuff people do.

948
00:43:31,800 --> 00:43:33,040
Cool stuff.

949
00:43:33,040 --> 00:43:34,560
Yeah?

950
00:43:34,560 --> 00:43:37,560
OK.

951
00:43:37,560 --> 00:43:38,480
Where was I with all that?

952
00:43:38,480 --> 00:43:39,120
OK, yeah.

953
00:43:39,120 --> 00:43:43,400
So yeah, that's another set of IPA symbols.

954
00:43:43,400 --> 00:43:45,120
These are your first IPA diacritics,

955
00:43:45,120 --> 00:43:47,520
I guess those little square doohickeys under the t

956
00:43:47,520 --> 00:43:48,680
and the d there.

957
00:43:48,680 --> 00:43:51,000
Those indicate that that particular t and d, those

958
00:43:51,000 --> 00:43:52,920
are dental.

959
00:43:52,920 --> 00:43:54,360
There are even languages out there

960
00:43:54,360 --> 00:43:57,840
that have both dental t's and alveolar t's.

961
00:43:57,840 --> 00:44:00,880
So the Dravidian languages of India

962
00:44:00,880 --> 00:44:02,280
are famous for having those.

963
00:44:02,280 --> 00:44:05,960
And a lot of the Aboriginal languages of Australia

964
00:44:05,960 --> 00:44:09,640
are really rich in places of articulation.

965
00:44:09,640 --> 00:44:12,080
So has Dinka come to think of it, this Nialotic language

966
00:44:12,080 --> 00:44:15,200
that I mentioned briefly that has tone as a way of marking

967
00:44:15,200 --> 00:44:17,280
case.

968
00:44:17,280 --> 00:44:19,120
These are all languages that have lots and lots

969
00:44:19,120 --> 00:44:23,040
of places of articulation, including both dental and alveolar.

970
00:44:23,040 --> 00:44:25,840
But that often don't make the languages of Australia,

971
00:44:25,840 --> 00:44:28,800
at least, often don't make voicing distinctions.

972
00:44:28,800 --> 00:44:30,120
So they have stops, but they don't

973
00:44:30,120 --> 00:44:32,160
distinguish voiced from voiceless.

974
00:44:32,160 --> 00:44:36,720
So they have places of articulation and lots of them.

975
00:44:36,720 --> 00:44:38,480
And nasals in all those places as well,

976
00:44:38,480 --> 00:44:42,240
but no distinction between voiced and voiceless.

977
00:44:42,240 --> 00:44:42,960
Yeah?

978
00:44:42,960 --> 00:44:45,600
OK, any other questions about this chart

979
00:44:45,600 --> 00:44:48,760
before we zoom past it?

980
00:44:48,760 --> 00:44:50,680
Like I say, I'm going to, on the website,

981
00:44:50,680 --> 00:44:54,680
there will be a link to charts that

982
00:44:54,680 --> 00:44:57,560
will look, hopefully, sort of like this,

983
00:44:57,560 --> 00:45:02,200
more official charts by the IPA, which will have sound files

984
00:45:02,200 --> 00:45:04,840
so that you can listen to trained phenologists making

985
00:45:04,840 --> 00:45:05,600
the sounds.

986
00:45:05,600 --> 00:45:08,880
And also, at least one website, which I hope is still up,

987
00:45:08,880 --> 00:45:12,200
has MRIs so that you can watch the inside of a person's

988
00:45:12,200 --> 00:45:16,160
vocal tract as they make the sound.

989
00:45:16,160 --> 00:45:18,680
OK.

990
00:45:18,680 --> 00:45:22,680
Now, we have been talking about parts of the vocal tract

991
00:45:22,680 --> 00:45:24,640
that English uses.

992
00:45:24,640 --> 00:45:26,520
Sometimes it doesn't use them for the same things

993
00:45:26,520 --> 00:45:27,440
that their languages do.

994
00:45:27,440 --> 00:45:28,160
So we have teeth.

995
00:45:28,160 --> 00:45:29,600
We use our teeth for intradentals,

996
00:45:29,600 --> 00:45:31,920
but we don't make dental stops.

997
00:45:31,920 --> 00:45:34,200
But there are places in the vocal tract

998
00:45:34,200 --> 00:45:37,920
that English just does not use, and yet, if other languages do.

999
00:45:37,920 --> 00:45:39,240
Here are a couple.

1000
00:45:39,240 --> 00:45:41,320
There are what are called retroflex sounds.

1001
00:45:41,360 --> 00:45:43,360
These are sounds in which the tip of your tongue

1002
00:45:43,360 --> 00:45:44,640
is on your palate.

1003
00:45:44,640 --> 00:45:50,040
So instead of a t, you're making a t, a ta, a ta.

1004
00:45:50,040 --> 00:45:52,360
So your tongue is curled back a little bit further

1005
00:45:52,360 --> 00:45:55,360
than it would be for a t, and it's making a closure

1006
00:45:55,360 --> 00:45:57,280
if you're making a stop right there.

1007
00:45:57,280 --> 00:46:00,200
So you can make stops there, a ta, or a da.

1008
00:46:00,200 --> 00:46:04,480
You can make fricatives there, like a sha, or a ja.

1009
00:46:04,480 --> 00:46:08,840
And you can make nasals there, like a na, a na.

1010
00:46:08,880 --> 00:46:14,000
Retroflex sounds are very popular in India and Australia

1011
00:46:14,000 --> 00:46:16,480
and Indonesia.

1012
00:46:16,480 --> 00:46:17,600
They're all a good place.

1013
00:46:17,600 --> 00:46:18,100
Yeah?

1014
00:46:18,100 --> 00:46:18,600
Yeah.

1015
00:46:18,600 --> 00:46:23,680
In the B, there's a retroflex lateral to it.

1016
00:46:23,680 --> 00:46:24,180
You're right.

1017
00:46:24,180 --> 00:46:24,680
Out of da.

1018
00:46:24,680 --> 00:46:25,180
Yeah?

1019
00:46:25,180 --> 00:46:26,400
Mm-hmm.

1020
00:46:26,400 --> 00:46:27,040
Yeah?

1021
00:46:27,040 --> 00:46:28,160
Well, we'll get to laterals.

1022
00:46:28,160 --> 00:46:30,680
But yes, yes, there is.

1023
00:46:30,680 --> 00:46:32,760
Yeah?

1024
00:46:32,760 --> 00:46:35,240
OK, there's retroflexes.

1025
00:46:35,280 --> 00:46:36,440
Uvulars.

1026
00:46:36,440 --> 00:46:40,160
Uvulars are kind of like k, except more so.

1027
00:46:40,160 --> 00:46:42,480
So for a k, your back of your tongue

1028
00:46:42,480 --> 00:46:45,120
is hitting the back of your mouth.

1029
00:46:45,120 --> 00:46:46,160
It's touching your velum.

1030
00:46:46,160 --> 00:46:48,320
That's a k sound, akka.

1031
00:46:48,320 --> 00:46:50,280
For a uvular, your tongue goes further back,

1032
00:46:50,280 --> 00:46:52,600
and it gets at or near your uvula,

1033
00:46:52,600 --> 00:46:53,800
which is the little doohickey that

1034
00:46:53,800 --> 00:46:57,120
hangs down there at the back.

1035
00:46:57,120 --> 00:46:58,120
That's your uvula.

1036
00:46:58,120 --> 00:47:04,080
So this is a sound like akka, akka, akka, or aga,

1037
00:47:04,080 --> 00:47:09,400
or agha, or agha, or agha.

1038
00:47:09,400 --> 00:47:12,880
These are all uvular sounds.

1039
00:47:12,880 --> 00:47:14,440
We do not have these in English, at least

1040
00:47:14,440 --> 00:47:15,360
when we're feeling well.

1041
00:47:15,360 --> 00:47:18,240
But there are languages out there that do.

1042
00:47:18,240 --> 00:47:23,160
You're working on a problem set that

1043
00:47:23,160 --> 00:47:25,480
involves the language inupiaq.

1044
00:47:25,480 --> 00:47:29,120
And that letter k is a symbol for a uvular stop.

1045
00:47:29,120 --> 00:47:31,040
Inupiaq has a uvular stop.

1046
00:47:31,040 --> 00:47:32,680
It has one at the end of the name.

1047
00:47:32,680 --> 00:47:34,320
The inu at the beginning means people,

1048
00:47:34,320 --> 00:47:36,080
and piaq is a suffix meaning something

1049
00:47:36,080 --> 00:47:39,320
like real, or normal, or regular.

1050
00:47:39,320 --> 00:47:41,560
So the inupiaq are the normal people.

1051
00:47:41,560 --> 00:47:43,040
The rest of us are abnormal.

1052
00:47:46,600 --> 00:47:51,040
So yeah, uvulars, reasonably popular.

1053
00:47:51,040 --> 00:47:52,640
They're all over, let's see, they're

1054
00:47:52,640 --> 00:47:56,600
all over the indigenous languages of the Americas.

1055
00:47:56,600 --> 00:47:59,440
They have them in Arabic.

1056
00:47:59,440 --> 00:48:05,320
They're not exactly rare, but English does not have them.

1057
00:48:05,320 --> 00:48:11,200
The uvular voiced fricative, so the voiceless fricative,

1058
00:48:11,200 --> 00:48:15,120
the ch, ch, is found.

1059
00:48:15,120 --> 00:48:19,840
But not usually common in Europe, the r, r,

1060
00:48:19,840 --> 00:48:22,880
is one of the voiced uvular fricative,

1061
00:48:22,880 --> 00:48:26,720
is one of the ways to pronounce an r in languages

1062
00:48:26,720 --> 00:48:28,360
like French and German.

1063
00:48:28,400 --> 00:48:31,360
So if you're pronouncing the French word for red,

1064
00:48:31,360 --> 00:48:33,640
one of the ways to do it is with this fricative to say,

1065
00:48:33,640 --> 00:48:40,200
r, r, that r sound is a voiced uvular fricative.

1066
00:48:40,200 --> 00:48:44,400
There are other ways you can also do a uvular trill,

1067
00:48:44,400 --> 00:48:49,360
where you get your uvula to flap in the breeze.

1068
00:48:49,360 --> 00:48:51,680
But not everybody does that.

1069
00:48:51,680 --> 00:48:54,080
Yep, questions about that?

1070
00:48:54,080 --> 00:48:54,560
Makes sense?

1071
00:48:55,280 --> 00:48:57,640
OK.

1072
00:48:57,640 --> 00:49:02,200
And then there are also pharyngeals.

1073
00:49:02,200 --> 00:49:06,880
Pharyngeals involve constriction near the pharyngeal wall.

1074
00:49:06,880 --> 00:49:08,600
Arabic has these.

1075
00:49:08,600 --> 00:49:09,760
The Berber languages have these.

1076
00:49:09,760 --> 00:49:13,800
These are like, ah, ha, ah, ah, ah, ah, ah.

1077
00:49:13,800 --> 00:49:15,200
So you're getting the back of your tongue

1078
00:49:15,200 --> 00:49:17,840
to get against the back of your vocal tract.

1079
00:49:17,840 --> 00:49:20,360
Does anybody here speak Arabic?

1080
00:49:20,360 --> 00:49:20,860
OK.

1081
00:49:20,860 --> 00:49:22,400
So if you know anyone who speaks Arabic,

1082
00:49:22,400 --> 00:49:23,640
get them to speak to you for a while,

1083
00:49:23,640 --> 00:49:25,320
and you'll get to hear them saying this.

1084
00:49:25,320 --> 00:49:26,920
As I say, there will be sound files

1085
00:49:26,920 --> 00:49:30,520
where you'll get to hear people making these.

1086
00:49:30,520 --> 00:49:35,400
So English doesn't have these, but they're languages that do.

1087
00:49:35,400 --> 00:49:36,920
There are pharyngeal fricatives.

1088
00:49:36,920 --> 00:49:39,280
Yeah, those are both pharyngeal fricatives.

1089
00:49:39,280 --> 00:49:42,960
OK, so slightly larger chart with more symbols on it,

1090
00:49:42,960 --> 00:49:44,680
including some of the ones we've talked about.

1091
00:49:44,680 --> 00:49:47,960
Pharyngeal tubulars, rectiflexes.

1092
00:49:47,960 --> 00:49:50,160
And for some reason, the dental stops are still red.

1093
00:49:50,160 --> 00:49:52,680
I don't know why to fix that.

1094
00:49:52,680 --> 00:49:56,120
Yep, there's a chart.

1095
00:49:56,120 --> 00:49:59,120
OK.

1096
00:49:59,120 --> 00:50:02,600
OK, now, people keep asking me about sounds

1097
00:50:02,600 --> 00:50:03,920
that I've been carefully avoiding,

1098
00:50:03,920 --> 00:50:06,080
so let's talk about them.

1099
00:50:06,080 --> 00:50:10,760
There are what are called approximants.

1100
00:50:10,760 --> 00:50:14,320
Approximants are not stops, and they're not fricatives,

1101
00:50:14,320 --> 00:50:15,720
and they're not nasals.

1102
00:50:15,720 --> 00:50:20,520
They involve your articulators kind of vaguely gesturing

1103
00:50:20,520 --> 00:50:24,360
towards each other in some part of your vocal tract.

1104
00:50:24,360 --> 00:50:27,640
Not enough to make, definitely not making contact,

1105
00:50:27,640 --> 00:50:30,560
and not enough to cause any turbulence in the air flow.

1106
00:50:30,560 --> 00:50:34,280
So if you think about a W, let's say,

1107
00:50:34,280 --> 00:50:37,640
in the middle of a word like away,

1108
00:50:37,640 --> 00:50:39,400
that's not a stop, and it's not a fricative,

1109
00:50:39,400 --> 00:50:40,360
and it's not nasal.

1110
00:50:40,360 --> 00:50:43,720
It's bilabial, you can feel your lips engaging

1111
00:50:43,720 --> 00:50:45,960
as you make the W in the middle of away.

1112
00:50:45,960 --> 00:50:49,280
It's a bilabial sound, but it's not a bilabial stop,

1113
00:50:49,280 --> 00:50:53,560
or a fricative, it is a bilabial approximate.

1114
00:50:53,560 --> 00:50:57,560
So similarly for the Y sound at the beginning of year,

1115
00:50:57,560 --> 00:51:03,520
or yard, and the L and the R at the beginning and end

1116
00:51:03,520 --> 00:51:11,360
of, I don't know, layer, or rail, those are all approximants.

1117
00:51:11,360 --> 00:51:16,520
They are sometimes divided into glides and liquids,

1118
00:51:16,520 --> 00:51:18,760
and I'm hoping that nobody will ask me

1119
00:51:18,760 --> 00:51:22,360
how you know whether something is a glide or a liquid.

1120
00:51:22,360 --> 00:51:24,160
Are you about to ask me how you know whether something

1121
00:51:24,160 --> 00:51:26,760
is a glide or a liquid?

1122
00:51:26,760 --> 00:51:28,640
Go ahead, is that what you're?

1123
00:51:28,640 --> 00:51:31,520
Yes, OK, all right, fine.

1124
00:51:31,520 --> 00:51:35,760
So a glide, I mean, sometimes it's as though a glide

1125
00:51:35,760 --> 00:51:39,760
is an approximate that if you were to hold it longer,

1126
00:51:39,760 --> 00:51:41,320
it would be a vowel.

1127
00:51:41,320 --> 00:51:47,560
So a W, if you just freeze yourself in W space,

1128
00:51:47,560 --> 00:51:49,720
you're making an OO, right?

1129
00:51:49,720 --> 00:51:52,080
A W is like an OO sped up.

1130
00:51:52,080 --> 00:51:53,840
We're going to talk about vowels later,

1131
00:51:53,840 --> 00:51:56,520
but if you think about what an OO is,

1132
00:51:56,520 --> 00:52:02,240
it's a held version of a W. And similarly, a Y sound,

1133
00:52:02,240 --> 00:52:06,920
yeah, is a sped up version of an E,

1134
00:52:06,920 --> 00:52:11,960
as opposed to an R or an L, which are just something else.

1135
00:52:11,960 --> 00:52:14,700
Because I have not given you a good way of distinguishing

1136
00:52:14,700 --> 00:52:16,800
glides from liquids, you can trust me

1137
00:52:16,800 --> 00:52:19,720
that I will never ask you to distinguish them in a way that

1138
00:52:19,720 --> 00:52:22,040
will make a difference for grades or anything like that.

1139
00:52:22,040 --> 00:52:23,840
You will never see me saying, no, you're wrong,

1140
00:52:23,840 --> 00:52:25,320
that's not a glide, it's a liquid.

1141
00:52:25,320 --> 00:52:30,720
I mean, I may say that, but there won't be minus 5 next to it.

1142
00:52:30,720 --> 00:52:31,720
Yeah?

1143
00:52:31,720 --> 00:52:37,040
I mean, if the W and the Y sound are both just short and

1144
00:52:37,040 --> 00:52:41,360
vowel sounds, why is it necessary to have these

1145
00:52:41,360 --> 00:52:45,560
as separate symbols, just like the CH having two letters?

1146
00:52:46,320 --> 00:52:47,680
That's a really good question.

1147
00:52:47,680 --> 00:52:52,320
So why don't we just use the letter OO for the bilabial

1148
00:52:52,320 --> 00:52:55,160
glide for the W?

1149
00:52:55,160 --> 00:52:58,680
And let me see if I can come up with a good answer to that.

1150
00:52:58,680 --> 00:53:01,680
There are, eventually, we're going to get to it.

1151
00:53:01,680 --> 00:53:05,640
So far, all we're doing is talking about sounds individually.

1152
00:53:05,640 --> 00:53:08,920
And eventually, we're going to start talking about sequences

1153
00:53:08,920 --> 00:53:10,600
of sounds.

1154
00:53:10,600 --> 00:53:14,760
And we're going to want to be able to talk about restrictions

1155
00:53:14,760 --> 00:53:18,280
on the ways in which sounds get to interact with each other.

1156
00:53:18,280 --> 00:53:20,040
And so it's going to be useful at that point

1157
00:53:20,040 --> 00:53:22,720
to be able to distinguish, for example, consonants

1158
00:53:22,720 --> 00:53:24,920
from vowels.

1159
00:53:24,920 --> 00:53:27,760
And once we do that, and you should

1160
00:53:27,760 --> 00:53:30,880
feel free to call me on this if it doesn't come up later,

1161
00:53:30,880 --> 00:53:33,320
I'll try to make sure it does, it'll

1162
00:53:33,320 --> 00:53:35,360
turn out to be useful to be able to think

1163
00:53:35,360 --> 00:53:38,800
of the consonant version of the sound, the W,

1164
00:53:38,800 --> 00:53:41,680
and also the vowel version of the sound, the OO.

1165
00:53:41,680 --> 00:53:44,160
But you're right, that there are plenty of sound changes

1166
00:53:44,200 --> 00:53:48,240
where you see them converting back and forth between each other.

1167
00:53:48,240 --> 00:53:50,080
That's the kind of thing that happens.

1168
00:53:50,080 --> 00:53:54,160
So yeah, but that's the prima facie reason

1169
00:53:54,160 --> 00:53:55,840
for distinguishing them, because there

1170
00:53:55,840 --> 00:53:59,960
are rules for the distribution of sounds

1171
00:53:59,960 --> 00:54:02,280
for which it's useful to have that distinction.

1172
00:54:02,280 --> 00:54:03,680
It's like anything else, I guess.

1173
00:54:03,680 --> 00:54:06,360
We make that distinction as long as it turns out

1174
00:54:06,360 --> 00:54:09,440
to be useful for explaining stuff.

1175
00:54:09,440 --> 00:54:10,280
Good question.

1176
00:54:15,120 --> 00:54:17,600
All right.

1177
00:54:17,600 --> 00:54:20,120
OK, so those are quite some liquids.

1178
00:54:20,120 --> 00:54:22,640
OK, and then, this was Raquel who

1179
00:54:22,640 --> 00:54:27,080
was asking about this before, sounds like ch and j.

1180
00:54:27,080 --> 00:54:31,560
I think I said you could think of ch as like a stop, a T,

1181
00:54:31,560 --> 00:54:36,920
followed by a fricative, ch, or j,

1182
00:54:36,920 --> 00:54:41,200
the j at the beginning and end of a word like judge,

1183
00:54:41,200 --> 00:54:47,240
as being a stop, a D sound, followed by a fricative, a j.

1184
00:54:47,240 --> 00:54:50,040
There's a little bit of a debate about whether what I just

1185
00:54:50,040 --> 00:54:51,760
said is the right way to think about this or not.

1186
00:54:51,760 --> 00:54:53,280
I think the standard way to think about it

1187
00:54:53,280 --> 00:54:54,520
is not exactly that.

1188
00:54:54,520 --> 00:54:58,280
What people do is say, yeah, there's this package deal,

1189
00:54:58,280 --> 00:54:59,720
an affricate.

1190
00:54:59,720 --> 00:55:02,040
So there's a name for this kind of thing.

1191
00:55:02,040 --> 00:55:05,240
An affricate is this kind of sequence,

1192
00:55:05,240 --> 00:55:08,520
something like a stop, followed by a fricative.

1193
00:55:08,520 --> 00:55:11,480
And the arguments about whether or not

1194
00:55:11,480 --> 00:55:13,400
to make this move, about whether to say, no, that's

1195
00:55:13,400 --> 00:55:16,440
a single sound with a complex motion,

1196
00:55:16,440 --> 00:55:18,720
as opposed to saying, no, there's two sounds

1197
00:55:18,720 --> 00:55:21,560
next to each other, has to do with the kinds of considerations

1198
00:55:21,560 --> 00:55:22,560
I was just talking about.

1199
00:55:22,560 --> 00:55:25,000
When you're trying to figure out what's the best theory of,

1200
00:55:25,000 --> 00:55:27,400
for example, what sequences of sounds

1201
00:55:27,400 --> 00:55:30,840
are allowed in a syllable in a given language,

1202
00:55:30,840 --> 00:55:32,760
sometimes it's useful to be able to say,

1203
00:55:32,760 --> 00:55:35,520
this is a language that doesn't ever, ever allow,

1204
00:55:35,520 --> 00:55:39,320
say, a syllable to end with a stop followed by a fricative.

1205
00:55:39,320 --> 00:55:41,800
Oh, but it's OK for it to end with ch, you know.

1206
00:55:41,800 --> 00:55:43,920
So we'll just treat that as a single package.

1207
00:55:43,920 --> 00:55:46,320
We won't treat it as a stop followed by a fricative.

1208
00:55:46,320 --> 00:55:50,400
That's the kind of move people make, yeah.

1209
00:55:50,400 --> 00:55:54,200
So terminology, OK?

1210
00:55:57,520 --> 00:55:59,480
All right.

1211
00:55:59,480 --> 00:56:02,720
So I've made the table whiter.

1212
00:56:02,720 --> 00:56:05,800
There are glides, like woof and yeh.

1213
00:56:05,800 --> 00:56:08,040
There are some other glides there,

1214
00:56:08,040 --> 00:56:09,320
which I can try to read to you.

1215
00:56:09,320 --> 00:56:12,120
What would a labiodental glide sound like?

1216
00:56:16,640 --> 00:56:18,560
What's a labiodental fricative?

1217
00:56:18,560 --> 00:56:21,240
A voiced labiodental fricative.

1218
00:56:21,240 --> 00:56:22,120
V, right?

1219
00:56:22,120 --> 00:56:23,840
Like a V sound.

1220
00:56:23,840 --> 00:56:26,880
So can you try to make a labiodental glide,

1221
00:56:26,880 --> 00:56:28,440
maybe between vowels?

1222
00:56:28,440 --> 00:56:32,680
It's going to be something like, a la, a la, a la.

1223
00:56:33,680 --> 00:56:34,800
English doesn't have those.

1224
00:56:34,800 --> 00:56:36,120
I believe Hindi does.

1225
00:56:36,120 --> 00:56:39,240
So there are languages out there that have labiodental glides.

1226
00:56:39,240 --> 00:56:43,800
Similarly, there are velar glides, a la, a la, a la,

1227
00:56:43,800 --> 00:56:47,200
where your tongue is just kind of vaguely gesturing

1228
00:56:47,200 --> 00:56:49,560
in the direction of your velum.

1229
00:56:49,560 --> 00:56:52,000
I kind of like the IPA symbol for the velar glide.

1230
00:56:52,000 --> 00:56:55,320
It looks like something out of Tolkien.

1231
00:56:55,320 --> 00:56:57,200
Yep.

1232
00:56:57,200 --> 00:57:00,200
OK.

1233
00:57:00,200 --> 00:57:01,560
All right.

1234
00:57:01,560 --> 00:57:04,440
So we've done consonants.

1235
00:57:04,440 --> 00:57:05,840
Had not done all the consonants.

1236
00:57:05,840 --> 00:57:08,280
So what I'm going to do is show you some vowels.

1237
00:57:08,280 --> 00:57:09,600
And then we'll circle back and look

1238
00:57:09,600 --> 00:57:12,400
at some particularly exotic consonants, probably next time,

1239
00:57:12,400 --> 00:57:13,760
probably not today.

1240
00:57:13,760 --> 00:57:15,920
I want to start talking about vowels.

1241
00:57:15,920 --> 00:57:18,920
So let's go through the vowels systematically.

1242
00:57:18,920 --> 00:57:21,960
Compare the vowel in the middle of bead

1243
00:57:21,960 --> 00:57:24,280
and the vowel in the middle of bad.

1244
00:57:24,280 --> 00:57:29,160
And here, let me just warn you that the IPA becomes

1245
00:57:29,160 --> 00:57:33,680
particularly forbidding when we get to vowels in English.

1246
00:57:33,680 --> 00:57:35,840
And it's not the fault of the IPA.

1247
00:57:35,840 --> 00:57:40,360
It's the fact that English has a very large number of vowels

1248
00:57:40,360 --> 00:57:44,760
and not a very good system for writing them.

1249
00:57:44,760 --> 00:57:47,040
This is one of the things that makes English spelling so

1250
00:57:47,040 --> 00:57:50,120
difficult that we can actually have competitions where you

1251
00:57:50,120 --> 00:57:54,800
watch people spell, this kind of thing that in many languages

1252
00:57:54,800 --> 00:57:55,920
would be impossible.

1253
00:57:55,920 --> 00:57:57,800
If you tried to do that in Finnish,

1254
00:57:57,800 --> 00:57:59,840
the spelling bead would just never end,

1255
00:57:59,840 --> 00:58:03,800
because every word is pronounced exactly the way it's spelled.

1256
00:58:03,800 --> 00:58:05,840
We don't do that in English.

1257
00:58:05,840 --> 00:58:09,960
So here are two vowels, and those are their IPA symbols,

1258
00:58:09,960 --> 00:58:12,760
the vowel in bead and the vowel in bad.

1259
00:58:12,760 --> 00:58:17,440
And now, if you, so everybody joined me in sort of transitioning

1260
00:58:17,440 --> 00:58:23,920
from one of those vowels to the other, go yeah, yeah, yeah.

1261
00:58:28,800 --> 00:58:31,200
OK.

1262
00:58:31,200 --> 00:58:33,440
You guys sound good.

1263
00:58:33,440 --> 00:58:35,600
What are you doing?

1264
00:58:35,600 --> 00:58:37,040
I mean, you're doing what I asked you to,

1265
00:58:37,040 --> 00:58:40,200
but what's happening in your mouth?

1266
00:58:40,200 --> 00:58:41,000
Yes?

1267
00:58:41,000 --> 00:58:42,720
The second vowel is more open.

1268
00:58:42,720 --> 00:58:44,120
Yeah, the second vowel is more open.

1269
00:58:44,120 --> 00:58:46,480
You're opening your mouth a little bit more.

1270
00:58:46,480 --> 00:58:49,520
What are you doing with your tongue?

1271
00:58:49,520 --> 00:58:51,360
Lowering from the top to the bottom.

1272
00:58:51,360 --> 00:58:52,440
Yeah, it's lowering.

1273
00:58:52,440 --> 00:58:53,680
It's going from the top to the bottom.

1274
00:58:53,680 --> 00:58:55,280
Yeah, I think you guys are both right.

1275
00:58:55,280 --> 00:58:58,720
If your tongue is sort of tensed and it's up there

1276
00:58:58,720 --> 00:59:03,320
at the top of your mouth, then for ah, your tongue drops.

1277
00:59:03,320 --> 00:59:06,760
In fact, it drops so far that it drags your jaw down with it.

1278
00:59:06,760 --> 00:59:08,760
Maybe there's a more reasonable way to say that.

1279
00:59:08,760 --> 00:59:11,040
You lower your jaw so that your tongue can

1280
00:59:11,040 --> 00:59:12,040
go even further down.

1281
00:59:15,040 --> 00:59:20,680
So one way of classifying vowels is in terms of height.

1282
00:59:20,680 --> 00:59:23,680
So we talk about the high vowel like heat

1283
00:59:23,680 --> 00:59:26,200
and the low vowel like hat.

1284
00:59:26,200 --> 00:59:29,320
And there are vowels in between, like the vowel in hate.

1285
00:59:29,320 --> 00:59:31,280
That vowel is called mid.

1286
00:59:31,280 --> 00:59:33,200
So we have high vowels, and we have mid vowels,

1287
00:59:33,200 --> 00:59:34,360
and then we have low vowels.

1288
00:59:40,000 --> 00:59:42,480
Yeah?

1289
